#passage(("If there's only one God, then that God has a description. So if you believe that God is the Trinity, Father, Son and Holy Spirit, then you will be praying to the Christian God. If you believe it's Krishna or Vishnu and all of these different things, you'll be a Hindu, cuz you're praying to a Hindu god. Hindus have loads of different gods. But the point I'm trying to make is this one God: it depends of your characteristics, the visualization you have in your mind; it will determine what you're praying to. So if you're praying to Creator, all powerful, all knowing, that you cannot visualize, that created everything and sends guidance to people, then that is a description of the Islamic God; that is not a description of the other God.", "Some people think Allah in the Arabic languages means god, the God, the Creator, the creator of everything. That's why the Christian Arabs use the same term, use the word Allah, because Allah means god. But that God has descriptions, the ones I'm telling you. If you agree with the descriptions, then you agree with the Islamic understanding of what God Is.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “3 Intelligent Christians Attempt To Challenge A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ys308Dw-w28", ((0, "0:00–0:41"), (53, "0:53–1:10"),), id: 455)
// U ML359:3-64 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("We use the term 'Allah'. Why do we use the term 'Allah'? Because the Arabic word 'Allah' is not masculine or feminine. It's uniquely for the Creator. So we believe Allah is uniquely One as well. He's not two, three, or four. In order for you to be the Creator, you have to be Absolute. In order for you to be God you need to be Absolute. In order for you to be Absolute you have to be One. So we believe Allah is uniquely One. We believe Allah is independent, meaning that Allah does not depend on anyone; everything depends on Allah. So everything in the universe depends on something else. For everything in the universe to depend on something else, there has to be something that's independent that everything depends upon. That's Allah. That's the Creator that everyone depends upon.", "And there's nothing like unto him, which means that anything I can conceive in my mind is not Allah. Allah is much greater than that. It's like saying 'look at the sun for 10 minutes straight', can you do it? No! Imagine the one who created the universe. Imagine the one who created the sun, the moon, the stars. And Allah says in the Quran 'Allah is the Light of the Heavens and the Earth' (Quran 24:35). Here you cannot even look at the sun; then imagine looking at the Light of the Heavens and the Earth! So that is the Creator that we believe in.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “University Student Challenges Muslim On Religion! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0eOxOwACYXE", ((16, "0:16–0:57"), (66, "1:06–1:32"),), id: 456)
