#passage(("Why do we need to know what God is? Because we need to know what we're worshiping. The one who made you and me, created everything, is God. Now the day I die and the day you die, if there is an afterlife and he's going to hold us accountable for what we do, we need to know who we're dealing with. If that Creator is the one who rewards, if he's the one who punishes, if he's the one who gives provision, if he's the one who looks after the universe and has control over it, then I need to know who I'm dealing with. Imagine going to a job, a very basic human example: working in a company, you don't know who the boss is, you don't know nothing about the boss, but you just working in a company, you work for them. There's consequences for you working in that company, so you need to have information and knowledge about who is running the [show], as they say. Now the one who controls everything and the one who holds everyone accountable is the creator; therefore it's very important to know.", "Let's say we don't know God and God is a ruthless Creator, that's a hypothetical example, that would punish everything, but I just don't know, so I'll live an evil life and then I'll get an infinite amount of punishment because I don't know. If I know that that creator for instance is all loving, all merciful, forgives everything, then I live the way I want to live because that Creator will forgive everything I do. So me knowing who the Creator is will reflect in my life. I need to know so I know how to behave in accordance towards that Creator. So it's very important that you should know. I think it's essential; it's a huge problem to follow any religion that does not have a clear definition of God.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Hindus Debate Muslim On The Rationality Of Hinduism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bsthKsAwd7o", ((123, "2:03–2:49"), (171, "2:51–3:29"),), id: 457)
// U ML355:839-855 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("The concept of God in Islam is very simple. It's a clear-cut separation between the creation and the Creator. There's no clearer cut separation between the creation and the Creator than in Islam. That's why the Quran is only the words of Allah, all words of the Creator. We don't put the words of Matthew, Mark, Luke, John, Sahabah صحابة Abu Bakr and Umar, we don't put these humans, their words with God's words, because they're different levels. Nothing can grasp of His Knowledge except that which He Wills. And there is nothing equivalent to Him. He does not become human, He does not become deficient, never dependent on food, never depending on sleep or drink. He does not do things that does not befits His Majesty, like what humans do after they eat the food.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Attempts To Show The Trinity In Scripture! - Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Uy7FdC-1AVk", ((2675, "44:35–45:38"),), id: 458)
// U ML204:231-303 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("Both me and you are disbelievers that God is a human being. We both disbelieve in Him. I also don't believe in that God. No Muslim believes that God is a human being! If you picture a human being in your head, that is not Allah by definition. What is the reason? The evidence is first, in the Quran Allah says: لَّا تُدۡرِكُهُ ٱلۡأَبۡصَـٰرُ وَهُوَ یُدۡرِكُ ٱلۡأَبۡصَـٰرَۖ (Vision does not encompass Him, and He encompasses all vision) (alAnām6:103). So vision, sight, in this life, you cannot encompass God. Allah says in the Quran: \"There's nothing like unto Him\" (ashShura42:11), لَیۡسَ كَمِثۡلِهِۦ شَیۡءࣱۖ وَهُوَ ٱلسَّمِیعُ ٱلۡبَصِیرُ (There's nothing like unto Him. And He's The All-Seeing, The All-Hearing).", "So when we think about Allah, we don't visualise anything, because we can't visualise it. We know that The Creator is beyond our simple experience of Creation, because what we observe is the Creation. So if God were it, He would be the Creation, He's what He created! He's not. He is unlike His Creation.", "So for instance, Allah describes Himself in the Quran as Light, but not the light that's coming right now. He's the light of everything, He's the light of guidance, the light of everything. Allah is a source of Beauty. He has Names and Attributes. This is what we think about when we think about Allah. He's The Most-Loving. He's The Most-Just. He's The Truth. He's Beautiful, and He's the Source of Beauty. He is All-Knowing. This is what we think about when we say God. But we do not picture any Creation or anything similar to the Creation. Now, we would say that it's polytheism to think, if anything in the Creation, I think this is God; this is disbelief straight away. If you think God is any of the Creation, you're not even a Muslim. You don't believe in Allah. That's not Allah.", "Now, when we talk about gender as well, to add on top of that, that also means that Allah is not bound by gender. We are male and female. That doesn't exist. Even things in Creation, many of them are not bound by gender. You're not going to call bacteria male and female. There are many things that you're not going to say male or female. So, Allah is The Creator of everything, and He is The One Who fashioned everything. This is The Creator that we believe in. So, when I tell you whatever created the universe, I'm referring to something that is supreme, transcendent, above everything and created all of this. Not some guy on a chair with a beard. We don't believe that. That's not The Creator that Muslims believe in. This is the Christian God.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Challenges Muslim On The Need For The Existence of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TCyh6BeSg50", ((770, "12:50–13:27"), (811, "13:31–14:04"), (848, "14:08–14:49"), (894, "14:54–15:37"),), id: 459)
