#passage(("I'm not trying to show you God exists. Me, as a Muslim specifically, my religion doesn't teach me to convince anyone. I personally believe or I personally know that the idea of God's existence is innate within every human being, and there is a lot of research that already established that. Oxford University conducted a study; people who are atheists, who speak on behalf of their own atheism, like Justin Barrett, conducted the experiment, 57 academics, and it was in a period of three years, and they brought children from different countries, and they concluded that the belief in God is innate. Every civilisation that existed in human history believed in some sort of a higher entity. They all had this idea in their mind; it's a part of their human existence and nature. For me, the movement of atheism is a new thing. So, this is why I think it's a part of human existence, and I've given this research or some idea of it. There's many other evidences as well.", "The description of God is very simple. Uniquely One, Independent, meaning everything else has to rely and depend on that thing, and Eternal. Does not beget nor is born, meaning it does not resemble the creation of the reproductive organs and having something before and something after it, because that would not make any rational sense.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Angry Atheist Challenges Muslim On Stream! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2l2wOowfqGg", ((881, "14:41–15:44"), (952, "15:52–16:06"),), id: 460)
// U ML097:26-65 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("You cannot just define God based on negations. If you just negate attributes from God, it's not enough to define God. For instance, I can say God is not bad. That doesn't necessarily mean he's good. So if I don't affirm goodness for God, that's an essential attribute for God's existence. I can say God is not ignorant. That doesn't necessitate that God is all knowing because he's not ignorant. So I find it very problematic. Negation is necessary, but in the same time affirmation is also necessary. So in an Islamic point of view, we only attribute to God what he attributes to himself, because Allah is the truth. For instance, one of his names, he's the all knowing, the most wise, all of these things. Whatever God commands you to do by default, that is good, because God himself is good. And what he forbids you from doing is by default a bad thing. God defines what good is and what bad is due to his perfect nature and existence. He possess that we don't possess.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Surprises Sceptical Chemist With The Evidences For Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z1ousrtPzAE", ((139, "2:19–3:40"),), id: 461)
// U ML211:304-418 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Looking at the universe tells us about the maker of the universe, so we derive the attributes of the maker of the universe through using the universe. For example, I use this table and I say whoever made it needed material, intelligence, knowledge of language. Looking at the universe you derive the attributes of God or that Creator or whatever made the universe. Can you create an Universe without ultimate power? No, because the universe is full of atoms, and if you [split] an atom you have a nuclear explosion from the amount of energy [stored] in each atom. You look at the laws; that requires an extensive amount of wisdom and knowledge, to know every small number of every detail of how the universe would expand, for example electrical magnetic waves, gravitational fields. All of these different things has to be independent, meaning before the universe, which means the time space [continuum] that we have, that restricts us, or we don't know the past or the future, is not going to apply to that entity the way applies to us. So then you start deriving the attributes of what people refer to as God essentially: so something that is existent before the universe, brought it into existence, powerful in a way we cannot comprehend, knowledgeable in a way we cannot comprehend; that is what we refer to as God, and otherwise the universe cannot exist, because you need the attributes of something to create.", "He describes himself in the Quran in chapter 112, and by the way, when I use the word he, describes, etc., we don't believe God is also restricted by gender the way we are, but this is language terminology. This is a creator that creates this world; it's unlike anything that we know, and that's naturally how things are. We don't create things which are the absolute identical existence of ourselves; we're always different. We make tables, we're not tables. We make chairs, we make objects; we make things that are usually other than ourselves. Creator created us; he's unlike us.", "But there are attributes or things that we would share. For instance we share knowledge, the idea of knowledge, not the same knowledge, but we share the idea of knowledge. The knowledge of God for example is eternal, meaning he always had the knowledge; we have it at the point of birth. Our knowledge is limited; the knowledge of the Creator is unlimited, he knows everything. Our knowledge is deficient, meaning we forget; the Creator does not forget. Our knowledge is not bound to hypothetical scenarios; like if someone didn't do something, God knows what would happen if he didn't do that thing that he didn't do, even though he didn't do it. So even a hypothetical scenarios: if you didn't come here today, Allah knows what would happen, the hypothetical scenario. So the knowledge of God extends to that level. We share the idea of knowledge, that we possess knowledge.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Examines The Muslim Belief In God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aymar_bEYMg", ((595, "9:55–11:06"), (685, "11:25–12:53"), (773, "12:53–13:36"),), id: 462)
// U ML208:224-266 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("I'm just using common sense now. God cannot be a created thing. Why? Because a created thing is in need of another entity external to it to create it. That's what a definition of a created thing is: something that was brought into existence by an external entity. If something is brought into existence by external entity, it cannot be God itself, because it's reliant on other than itself to exist, and whatever brought it into existence can determine whether it exists or not. Then that is the reality what God is: that which created it and allowed it to exist to begin with.", "When Prophet Muhammad came to the [Bedouin] Arabs, they were worshiping Idols as well. They had idols and they said the Idols are not God, like the Hindus would say the Idols are not God, is the image of whatever it is. Prophet Muhammad came to them and said these Idols are of dead people, images of God, whatever it is; these just rocks and images that you created with your own hand. They don't speak, they don't benefit, they don't harm, they cannot influence you in any way. You should [worship] the one who created them and you. Instead of worshiping that which is made, you worship that which made everything, the creator of everything.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Hindu Questions Muslim On Polytheism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Nbn5J51AtE", ((503, "8:23–8:55"), (543, "9:03–9:36"),), id: 463)

