#passage(("What do you mean \"we're made in God's image\"? Because that statement can be true in a way, false in a different way. I need to know what you mean by that statement, because different Christians have different beliefs. Someone would say \"Made in God's image, Jesus is God, he was a human being, we're human\". It just depends what you mean by \"We're made in God's image\". If you say what you mean is that human beings possess attributes, God possesses attributes, there is a link in the definition of what that attribute is: we have Love, the Creator has Love; we have Knowledge, the Creator has Knowledge; but the way the Creator has them and the way we have them is different. God has absolute knowledge, full knowledge, eternal knowledge, etc. We have limited knowledge, starts at some point, it's not eternal.", "If that's what you mean, Muslims believe the same thing. We don't have any issues saying that we have the attribute of love, the Creator has the attribute of love; God's love is maximally perfect, our love is deficient, etc. We don't have any issues with saying that, if this is the meaning of the statement. Then the meaning you're saying we don't have an issue with, but we wouldn't use this statement in a way that can confuse people. What do you mean \"image\"? You mean that God looks exactly like we look? Then we say: \"We reject that meaning, we don't accept that meaning\".", "But in your belief, you believe Jesus was God and Jesus was a human. So that statement will be literal in that sense. So depending on what Christian you are, if you believe that Jesus was God, that Jesus was human, then you are in that image exactly, that image of God. Same image. Literally now, physically talking, not just attributes or any of that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Attempts To Explain The Concept Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y0EuiG8SUZM", ((23, "0:23–1:29"), (94, "1:34–2:01"), (129, "2:09–2:53"),), id: 487)
