#passage(("Is Allah عز وجل in the السماء? Is Allah عز وجل above His Throne? Allah عز وجل says in surah Al-Mulk \"ءَأَمِنتُم مَّن فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ\" (Quran67:16). This is one example. What does السماء mean in the Arabic language? السماء means \"that which is above\". This is the problem: in understanding the language of the Arabs, we understand the Quran. The word السماء is used for that which is above.", "Where is the evidence? I'll give evidence from the Quran. Allah عز وجل said السحاب (the clouds) is between the سماء and the أرض, is between the Heavens and the Earth. Allah says: \"وَٱلسَّحَابِ ٱلْمُسَخَّرِ بَيْنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ\" (Quran2:164). And the سحاب, that is مسخر, that is guided between the السماء and between the Earth. So, Allah has stated that the سحاب is between the سماء and the [أرض], so it's not in the سماء. It's between. Then Allah says in another verse \"وَیُنَزِّلُ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَاۤءِ مَاۤءࣰ\" (Quran30:24): He brings down [from] the سماء water. The سماء here is referring to the سحاب. So, it is called سماء because it's above. So the Arabs used the word سماء for anything that is above.", "So, when Allah عز وجل says في السماء, it means in Highness, in aboveness. Allah is above. If you want to use the word في to mean \"in\", the word في in the Arabic language also comes with the meaning of the word upon, على. How do we know that? Allah says that in the Quran. Allah عز وجل says لأُصلّبنّهم, quoting what فرعون (Pharaoh) said about the Magicians: \"وَلَأُصَلِّبَنَّكُمْ فِي جُذُوعِ النَّخْلِ\", I'll crucify you \"on\" the palm trees. But it uses the word في. في comes in the Arabic language with the meaning على. So, he's not going to put them in the trees. He's going to put them \"on\" the trees. But in the Arabic language we use في with the meaning of word على. It's not context. It's understanding the language of the Arabs. So, this verse here states 2 things, in either way you want to understand it: it either says that Allah is \"above the Heavens\" or it says that Allah is \"in Highness\". Whichever understanding you want to take, both of them mean the same thing.", "What does استوى mean according to Bukhari and [Abu al-’Aliyah]? Who's better? Ibn Hajar or al-Bukhari? And he's إمام أهل الحديث (the Leader (Imam) of the Specialists of Hadith). Al-Bukhari is above him. So, looking at Bukhari now, before we go to Ibn Hajar, which was already influenced by Asharis, so I'm not talking about him. I'm talking about Bukhari, which we agree is from the Salaf. He's from the Salaf. He took directly from the students of the صحابة (Companions). He's among the students of the صحابة. There is no comparison. He's from the generations that are favoured by the Prophet ﷺ.", "So, al-Imam al-Bukhari, what did he say about استوى? When he brought the أثر of Abu al-’Aliyah, who's from the Salaf, what did he say استوى means? He said علا وارتفع. He rose above. \"استوى على\". And استوى, whenever it is used in the Quran, in any place with the word على (استوى by itself has many meanings, that's correct), but when it's in conjunction with على, it means \"above\". You can go to any place in the Quran: \"إِذَا اسْتَوَيْتُمْ عَلَيْهِ\" (Quran43:13), when you rise above.", "I didn't use the word \"move\". I used words in the Quran. Allah rose. I used the terms that the Salaf (Predecessors) use: He rose. I just wanted to show one thing: that the أشاعرة say things but they cannot stand on their leg when they talk. I said to you what al-Bukhari says or what Abu al-’Aliyah says, and you call them Salafis. If they are Salafis, then Ok I'm Salafi like them, what's the problem?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aggressive Ashari Interrogates Muhammed Ali On Stream!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7SGfzzryjY", ((1297, "21:37–22:34"), (1354, "22:34–23:01"), (1387, "23:07–23:51"), (1443, "24:03–24:52"), (1502, "25:02–25:25"), (1530, "25:30–26:08"),), id: 494)
