#passage(("First thing is, out of manners, let's say you seek knowledge from someone important; you don't say what's your evidence, or generally with your brothers you say what's the evidence for. It's very important, because it seems accusative when you say to someone what's your evidence. You said, in the principle that we said, what is the evidence that we affirm the text apparent meaning. That is a question asked to the person who doesn't affirm things in their apparent meaning, because the default position is everything taken is apparent meaning. The default position is if someone is told something, he doesn't take it metaphorically; he takes it literally; he understand it the way it is. So to come to someone and say give me evidence for why we take things the way they are makes no sense. What we would demand is evidence for those who would say don't take things the way they are. So that's the first thing we understand.", "Allah says I'm near. Why do we say that Allah near does not mean Allah is in the creation? The answers are many. Because the text of the Quran and Sunnah as we were given, we said that we take things in their apparent meaning unless there's evidence from the Quran and Sunnah that takes us away from that. In this case there's evidence from the Quran and Sunnah; there's more than 1,000 evidence that we can have, for example, that Allah is above, and there are tons of evidence; I can go on and on and on. The evidences are countless. So in his actions and his statements, in the Quran, in the Sunnah, and in the consensus of the companions, in the consensus of the salaf, they affirm that Allah is above. You have [Imam] Malik; Malik says that; you see that from the consensus on this issue.", "You can refer, for example, to all of the books of the righteous predecessors; any book you will bring, you will see bringing the statements of the righteous [predecessors], and they say in the beginning of these books: this is the beliefs. They didn't say this is my beliefs; this is the beliefs of all the scholars in all of the Cities. They don't say this is my belief; they say that we've been to Sham, and they mention all of the areas as well, and then they say that they affirm that Allah is above. So now we know that Allah is above, with all of these evidences and consensus and agreed upon. So we know here near cannot mean that, near meaning that Allah is within the creation, and because also we have clear explanations from the companion explaining these same verses, saying that it means about knowledge. We are not afraid as the people of the truth. If you're upon the truth you have no problems with answering questions.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ULnwXbVf_cM", ((9492, "2:38:12–2:39:06"), (9548, "2:39:08–2:40:31"), (9631, "2:40:31–2:41:44"),), id: 495)
// U ML388:488-512 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("The biggest problem that Muslims have is that all religions do not focus on God; they focus on people or other things. Focus on Jesus, focus on Buddha, focus on a country, India, you call it Hinduism, a culture. Focus on everything but not God! Islam, the first and last thing you have to focus on is God. The word \"Muslim\" means to submit to The Creator. Muslim is to submit to God. Allah says if they ask you about Me, indeed I am near, I answer the call of the person (Albaqara2:186). So Allah is saying straight away, I'm near. If you call upon me, I call upon you.", "How close is He when He says near? Allah says in the Quran as well, He's closer to us than our jugular vein (Qaf50:16). Allah is saying He's closer to you than your jugular veins, which means He's so close to you that anything you need, Allah doesn't need a secretary. No one is: \"Please, can I see God now? Is He free?\" No! Go to God straight away. This is The Message of Islam; it's التوحيد (Tawhid Monotheism), One Creator, worship Him alone, give Him absolute glorification, seek absolute help from Him. Do not do that to any other human beings, even Prophet Muhammad! A man said to Prophet Muhammadﷺ: \"Whatever Allah wills AND you will\". Heﷺ said: \"no, don't say that; say what Allah wills THEN you will later on. But don't put me equal with God\".",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Sincere Christians Investigate Islam Part 2! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lC7m3Z_Ms-U", ((1249, "20:49–21:17"), (1281, "21:21–21:54"),), id: 496)
