=== 7.8 Principles concerning Allah's names and attributes
// NODE P02.C01.S08
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML239:1168-1190 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("The last thing to believe in: the names and attributes of Allah. Allah has names, Allah has attributes. The names of Allah that we said, the 99 names, the ones that we're told have attributes. Allah is the wise; the attribute there is wisdom. We affirm the name, that Allah his name is Alim, and the attribute, that he is all wise. We affirm that Allah has absolute strength, so we affirm the name and the attributes.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ULnwXbVf_cM", ((2719, "45:19–45:53"),), id: 497)
// U ML239:1712-1901 | The Muslim Lantern | 8
#passage(("Now the people of the truth, the Muslims (all of these are terms that you can use for the people of the truth, those who believe the same things the Companions and the righteous predecessors believed), they have 10 principles for the names and attributes of Allah.", "The first principle of these 10 principles when it comes to the names and attributes of Allah is that the names and attributes of Allah are based on the Quran and Sunnah text. Where we derive knowledge about Allah, his names and his attributes, is strictly from the Quran and Sunnah, from the text. Meaning you cannot now think of a new name of Allah or a new attribute of Allah, you start telling us this is the name of Allah, it's an attribute of Allah. You cannot do this.", "The scholars say: if you want to know the attributes of something, there are only three ways for you to know that. First is that you see that thing, so you'll know how the attributes of that thing works or what is the attributes of that thing. Second thing is you see something exactly identical to it, 100%; that will help you know the description of that thing. Just like if I see a human being, now I have a description of what a human being is: something that is identical to it. Or you're told by a truthful source that is 100% reliable and there is no doubt about the authenticity of it, and they tell you about that. These are the only ways that you can know about something's description.", "So when it comes to the names of Allah, Allah tells us in the Quran: they cannot encompass of his knowledge except that which he wills. Allah says that vision does not encompass him and he encompasses all vision. All of these texts shows you that we cannot fully understand Allah or know about Allah except that which he tells us. That's why Allah says they do not encompass of his knowledge except the way that he wills, which is through the messengers, through the prophets; he tells us about himself. That's how we know about Allah names and attributes.", "Now what about the companions? Because we know there are some narrations of the companions that talks about the attributes of Allah and names of Allah. We say, when it comes to the companions, where are the companions going to bring knowledge from? The companions are not going to speak about Allah without knowledge. They're not like us today, or many of us today, let's say. That's why this is a rule in general, which is: when there is a authentic narration attributed to a companion and he doesn't attribute it to the prophet, meaning the companion statement, but it speaks about the unseen, then it has the same ruling as if he attributed to the prophet, because that companion is not going to speak about Allah without knowledge.", "Why? Because we know how Allah speaks about speaking about him without knowledge. Allah mentions the things that he made haram, and to transgress without right. Is there anything harder than [shirk]? But Allah mentions that you speak about Allah without knowledge after he mentioned [shirk], even though we know there is no greater sin, to emphasize its importance: that you should not be speaking of Allah without knowledge in any way, under any situation. Because based on the other verses that we just said, that no one can encompass Allah in knowledge, which means you would lie about Allah. Who is more [unjust] than the one who makes up a lie about Allah?", "So it's very important. Companions understand this more than any of us, and they understand also that Allah says: for Allah is the most beautiful or most perfect of names, so call upon Him using them; leave those who deviate in them. So the [companions] are not going to deviate; they will be hold into account with that which they used to do.", "Therefore we say the companions also, anything that they say that we have, and the agreed upon righteous [predecessors] as well, the one that were agreed upon by their communities, like [Imam] Ahmed for example. They derived their knowledge from the [sahabah] and they derived their knowledge from the Quran. So that's a principle that we have: if we have the sahabah and we have the righteous predecessors describing things, we also take that, to do with the beliefs that we have. But specifically the names and attributes, it's only the Quran and Sunnah, and you will have the statements of the [sahabah], because these are the names: you affirm this is a name of Allah, this is attribute of Allah. So that's the first.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ULnwXbVf_cM", ((4083, "1:08:03–1:08:19"), (4108, "1:08:28–1:09:04"), (4150, "1:09:10–1:10:06"), (4206, "1:10:06–1:10:57"), (4257, "1:10:57–1:12:03"), (4326, "1:12:06–1:13:10"), (4393, "1:13:13–1:13:42"), (4434, "1:13:54–1:14:45"),), id: 498)
// U ML239:1985-2823 | The Muslim Lantern | 32
#passage(("The third rule is that the attributes of Allah are all attributes of perfection. Why? We mentioned the verse of the Quran where Allah says the most perfect of attributes. One of his names means the one who's reached the maximum level of honor, the maximum level of [perfection]; his name is greater than anything else. If these are the descriptions of Allah, then all of his attributes are attributes of perfection, because you cannot be the most great if some of your attributes are attributes of deficiency. Meaning, if the Quran or [Sunnah] say any attribute, you hear the prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam described Allah with attribute X or the Quran describes Allah with X, then it is an attribute of perfection, whether your mind understands this idea or not. That's irrelevant: what sounds nice to you, or what your mind thinks, my deficient or your deficient mind thinks.", "Does Allah know himself more or we know Allah more? Some groups of misguidance believe they know Allah more than Allah knows himself. They wouldn't say it openly, but they will say: this text actually, we cannot take it in its apparent meaning; we cannot take these verses. So you know more than Allah? You're going to give a better way of explaining the verses of Allah? Essentially what you're saying: Allah is not able to clearly convey information and you are going to convey information better than him. And they don't say this in one or two attributes; they say this in most of the attributes, if not all of the attributes. So these are people of misguidance.", "The rules of the people of the truth is: we believe all the attributes of Allah attributes of perfection. If Allah described himself with it or the prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam described him with it, then we accept it, because the Hadith of the Prophet is the same as the Quran when it comes to it being revelation, from this point; we're not saying in every point. Both of them are revelation. Prophet sallallahu alhi wasallam, he said: I was given the Quran and something similar to it, meaning another revelation with it, referring to the Hadith. Allah says he does not speak of his own; it's only revelation given to him. So that means that if the prophet describes Allah with a name or an attribute, it is an attribute of perfection. You take that as a general rule. You say: Allah said it, I believe it, done.", "Number four: the scholars have principles when it comes to the names of Allah, his names and attributes. What are these principles? We affirm to Allah what he affirmed to himself. First thing we say is: without negation, negating this attribute completely of Allah; and without deviation, we don't deviate in this attribute, we change its meaning, we play around with what it means; and without saying that Allah his [attribute] is 100% the same, identical to an attribute of the creation. Someone who says Allah's face is like my face is a disbeliever with the consensus of all the scholars. Why? Allah says in the Quran: there's nothing like unto Allah. Finishing the verse is very important: and he is the all hearing, allseeing. So if you're hearing and seeing, Allah is hearing and seeing, is it the same way? So we do not say that is the same way, the exact same way. These are the principles that we have.", "And also, when it comes to the [howness]: Allah does certain actions. Allah hears all our duas in the same time. How many people are making dua in the same second? Countless, probably cannot count. Allah hears all of them the same time, understands them in the same time, in every language. Can someone sitting down here tell us how exactly? No. So when it comes to how Allah's attributes operate, we say we do not know. Meaning we affirm that Allah hears all the sounds, but how Allah does it we don't know.", "We believe that Allah descends; he descends in the last third of the night. It's mass transmitted, but as we said, regardless, it doesn't matter. It says our Lord descends. Do you have another Lord? Because some groups, they believe that Lord is an angel. So when it says our Lord descends, and then that Lord that descends says: is there anyone who has a need, so I can give him that need? Is anyone seeking forgiveness, so I can forgive him? Do you know of an angel that gives every need and forgives? No. We know only there's one Lord that does this. So Allah descends. Now someone comes and says, how does Allah descend? I'll say, I told you two minutes ago, we do not know how Allah attributes operate, because we said a second before that you need to either see something, see something like it, or be told from a truthful source, if you've not observed that thing yourself.", "I'll give a simple example. He narrates from one of the righteous predecessors; he mentions there's a narration, an event happened, where he heard (by the way, died 135 ah, very early, from the righteous predecessors) that some of the people of influence, one of them, was speaking about Allah names and attributes in an incorrect way. He was negating; he was doing the things that we said you cannot be doing; he's speaking about how these attributes are in reality. Then he went and he talked to him and he asked him: let's agree on this principle. He said the principle is: if we cannot know the attributes of the creation, how they are, can we know the attributes of the Creator?", "We all [know Jibril] has how many wings? 600. So we know of two wings. He said to him, I'm not going to ask you about the 597, but tell me where is the third one? So now I want you to picture: you have [Jibril], it's a creation; every wing closes the horizon, as the prophet described. So now we're seeing 600 wings. Where is the third wing? Where is it going to be? We have two; we know how two wings are like, we see in the creation. So he said to him (he submitted): if you cannot know even the attributes of the creation, how do you expect to know the [attributes] of the Creator, how they are?", "But do we understand what a wing is? Yes. So we can understand the meaning, and this very important: we can understand the meaning of an attribute without understanding how it is in reality. So since this is the case, when it comes to Allah, same thing: there are things that we can understand their meaning, but we don't understand how they are in reality. We know Allah is hearing and seeing, but we do not know how Allah is able to hear everything and see everything. We know that Allah has a face that befits him; we know that Allah has hands. We know the meaning of the word face; we know the meaning of the word hand; but we do not know how they are in relation to Allah: we've not seen Allah, we've not seen something like Allah, and the prophet has not told us how they are.", "So also, by the way, the angels, very few examples: the angels also have hands; their hearts, this is in the Quran. Are their hearts like the one that pumps blood here? No. So we understand what heart means, but we do not understand how this applies to the creation, the angels.", "This is now number five: affirming the existence of the attribute, but we do not affirm the [howness], which is what we were just explaining. We affirm the existence; so we say Allah has a hand, Allah has a face, Allah has any of these attributes that we describe: Allah is all hearing, Allah is all seeing, Allah is above everything. All of these attributes we [affirm], but we do not delve into the [howness] of the attribute. Why do you stop and say: cut this completely, we don't even think about knowing how it is? Why? We're not told. The three things I told you: we've not seen Allah, we've not seen something like Allah, and we not told.", "So no matter how much you try to think: you couldn't even know your own attributes as a human being. If I tell you to tell me the intricate details of your brain, your own brain, you can't. How are you expecting to know a [howness] of Allah's attributes? You cannot know yourself or the creation, let alone the creator of everything.", "Number six: these attributes of Allah are known from one aspect and unknown from another aspect, so also emphasizing what we were describing. Meaning we know the meaning of the attributes that were described: describing highness, we know what highness means; we know what rise above means; we know what hand means; we know what descend means. So this is the part that is known, but the part that is unknown is the [howness].", "[Imam] Malik: the man entered upon him in the very famous narration. Rise above: that's one of the meanings; there are four meanings. So he said to him that Allah is rose above. Now the people of misguidance misuse this narration, because he asked [Imam] Malik about the [howness]: how did Allah rise above the throne? And you were just talking about the impossibility of knowing that. So [Imam] Malik, of course, after reclining his head, thinking, and started to sweat, and he got angry, but then he said that statement that the Muslims are upon, because that statement is upon what the people who came before [Imam] Malik were upon, and the people come after [Imam] Malik would remain upon.", "The meaning of it is known in the language; there's no one who doesn't know what it means. We get some of the people: what does hand mean? You don't know what hand mean? I'll take you to the mental hospital. You're ignorant to that extent. We don't need to talk about Allah's names and attributes. So this is the more authentic narration: the [howness] is not comprehendable; you cannot comprehend it as a limited creation that you are; it's not in your capacity, so whatever I tell you doesn't benefit you anyways. You have to believe. And to ask about it (not the attributes, you can ask about the attributes, but to ask about how these attributes are), it's an innovation. And then he was kicked out: take him out, kick him out of the Masjid.", "Number seven: we affirm the text of the Quran and the Sunnah upon its apparent meaning. This is a very important one. What does it mean, apparent meaning? Meaning if I say this brother's hand is injured, would anyone here think that that means he's weak? But if I say to you this brother's hand is injured and he has no arms, it could mean that his assistant, his right hand (because we use the word hand to mean you're close by) is injured. It could mean that he's weak, if you want to take the hand mean here. Why? Because now the apparent meaning is not applicable; I told you that this person has no arms, no hands, so it's not applicable to say that his actual hand.", "So when it comes to the names and attributes of Allah, we take them in their apparent meaning. We do not deviate from the apparent meaning except, as the same example I told you, there is evidences that forces you to go somewhere else. And what is the evidences that forces you to go somewhere else? Your intellect, your mind, it doesn't sound nice to you, so that's evidence? What is it? Quran and Sunnah. It's something in the Quran and Sunnah, or the consensus of the companions, that would tell us what is intended here is not the apparent meaning. It's a principle that we affirm things in language in their apparent meaning. This is the case with everyone; that's why I give an example and everyone agreed. No one here disagreed, say no, we understand it means strength; we understand it means his assistant, because this is how language works.", "And if you say this is not the case (now we're saying the hypothetical situation, which what the people of misguidance do), that we do not take the apparent meaning, and we deviate into which meaning we want. So when Allah doesn't mean that he rose, it means he took over the throne. Why? Because you know what rise means: rise, a place, space. Quran and Sunnah? No, this is like: it doesn't make sense to me, and the Quran [and Sunnah] have to make sense to me. Me? Who are you, man? The righteous Companions and the righteous predecessors, are you better than them? Did any of them come to the prophet and say, I don't understand this, explain it to me? They submitted to it, because they understood the principles that were setting forth: the [howness] is not something that you can comprehend and access; therefore they stopped. So we take the apparent of the text of the Quran and Hadith.", "So when the prophet sallallahu alaih wasallam he says that Allah laughs, we affirm that. Do you know what laughing means, or do we need to go to the mental hospital? We know what laughing means, but do we know how Allah laughs? We do not know how Allah laughs. Now the people of misguidance, they will pretend they follow the same principle, but they don't. This is what separates the people of truth from the people of falsehood: they don't negate the [howness] like we do, which we have no problem with; they negate the actual meaning of laughing: laughing, it doesn't mean laughing.", "For example, they say Allah's Mercy means that he bestows upon you favors, he gives you money; that's what Mercy means. But do you know what Mercy means? Does mercy mean I give you a gift? But this is what they understand apparently: Mercy means I give you a gift, because they say Mercy is like a human attribute, all of this stuff. Quran and Sunnah clearly stipulate Mercy is an attribute of Allah. We affirm Mercy; we understand what Mercy means in the language. And the problem, if we don't do that, is that we will end up with saying that Allah communicates to us in a way in which you cannot comprehend. Meaning if I start telling you: do you understand Kish, as they say, or no? Now they believe the Quran and sunah is the same as what I just told you, because they say we cannot understand the meaning of these attributes in the Quran.", "The next one, which is number eight: that we stick to the terminology, and we avoid any terminologies which have correct meaning and false meaning. So there are certain words, certain terms, certain phrases that people would use that actually mix between the truth and falsehood, and this is the position of the people of falsehood; that's what they do: they use terms which contain both correct meaning and incorrect meaning, these general terms that they use. People of the Sunnah stick to the terms of the Quran and Sunnah and the clear-cut terms that do not contain any falsehood within them.", "For example, the people of the Quran and the Sunnah see Allah say in the Quran: Allah hears; anything that Allah says in the Quran, you affirm it the way it is. Now if someone comes and says Allah is not in a place, now let's ask this question: is there any text from the Quran and Sunnah that says this? No.", "So this is now a term that is a general term. Do you know why it's a general term? Because someone can say Allah is not in a place. What do you mean by a place? You mean something that restrict him and his attributes, makes him deficient? There is a correct meaning. We say that Allah is not in that; we don't say place, we say that meaning that you said, not the word, the meaning. So that meaning is negated. But is Allah above? Is Allah in the [sama]? We say Allah is in the [sama], but they would use the term Allah is not in place, so they can negate that Allah is in the [sama], not the other meaning that we agree we should negate.", "So that is a general term, and the people of falsehood use these general terms to manipulate the lay public: a general term not in the Quran and Sunnah that can either mean truth or falsehood. That's a very important thing to keep in mind. Allah is not this, Allah is not that: what is the Quran that says that? Show me text, or the [sahabah], as we said. Where is that? If they cannot show you that, then you say: you're making this up. You can use fabricated narrations; anything you use, we look at the chains, for anything that they will present forward.", "Now number nine: the default position in knowing Allah, in knowing his names and attributes, is affirmation, not negation. We do not know Allah through negating things; we know him first through affirming. We affirm Allah's attributes. You will see the people of falsehood, they start with negation: Allah is not this, Allah is not that. If I ask you, tell me about your friend (let's say you have a friend called Omar), you say Omar is not tall, Omar is not handsome, Omar does not go to school. I said who is Omar; I want to identify. Don't tell me Omar is not; what is Omar?", "So the people of misguidance, this is what they do: they affirm Allah's attributes by negation, which is why they have many attributes which are not in the Quran [or Hadith]. It came from someone's pocket. If you open their books, they start with negation. We affirm for Allah first; affirmation first. We don't just do negation and leave affirmation; we do both: we affirm for Allah, he affirmed for himself, and we negate the opposite of these attributes, the deficiencies that he negated from himself.", "The last thing is that these names and attributes of Allah are divided: the scholars have divided some of these names and attributes of Allah into different sections, based on certain principles. In the previous lecture someone asked about where did you get this number one, number two, number three, this kind of way of distinguishing things. We explained to them that they look at the Quran and Sunnah, and they try to make things easy for you by giving you principles, number one, number two, number three. But is the meaning in these principles correct or not, is it based on the Quran and Sunnah, is what you should ask; not why you giving me number one, number two, why you making it into these types of attributes, that type of attributes. That's not how it works.", "What the scholars do when they need to make these distinctions is because the people of falsehood, as we said, mix the truth with the falsehood by using general terms, by mixing things together. As Allah says: do not mix the truth with the falsehood. But whatever principles the people of the truth will make, it will be based on the Quran and Sunnah and evidences in the Quran.", "For example, they divide Allah attributes into attributes of action, attributes of essence, because the actions is not the same as the essence. So they divide the attributes of Allah in that way, into attributes of action, attributes of essence, and attributes of affirmation, attributes of negation. For example, just like we saying Allah negated certain things for himself and he affirmed certain things for himself; this is scholar divide into this way, these attributes of affirmation and attributes of negation. So these are the principles of the people of the truth when it comes to the names and attributes of Allah.", "Before finishing I want to mention the statement of one of the righteous predecessors, [Nu'aym ibn] Hammad, who died 228 ah, who said: whoever likens Allah in every way to his creation has committed [disbelief]. So now I'm just showing you the affirmation of everything we were just saying. Whoever likens Allah in every way to his creation, saying Allah's [attribute] is like mine, and whoever negates what Allah has described himself with, he has committed [disbelief]. So here he speaks first, then he speaks about those who do negation in every way, and says that these two things are disbeliefs. And he says: in what Allah described himself with or his messenger described him with, there is no likening between the two; doesn't exist. If Allah describes himself with something, there's no likening there. And this statement is mentioned with one of the strongest [isnad] out there, authentically attributed.", "So this is what we believe. These principles are based on the Quran and Sunnah. These are the principles that we apply, that we believe, that we take with ourselves to the grave, because what is the first question in the grave? Who's your lord. You have three questions; the first one is: you need to know who's your lord before you ask about what is your religion and your Prophet. So these principles we need to understand.", "I'm just giving you the torch, and you take the torch and you go and you apply it by studying and learning. I left now the other articles of Faith; now it's upon you to go and learn the details of these other things that we've not talked about, and more detail in what we talked about, which is belief in Allah. Of course believing in Allah is the most important, but every single one of them is needed, and if you're missing a single one cannot be a Muslim. If you don't believe in one of them, you're not a Believer; to believe in all of them to be from the Believers.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ULnwXbVf_cM", ((4673, "1:17:53–1:19:18"), (4758, "1:19:18–1:20:01"), (4801, "1:20:01–1:21:01"), (4881, "1:21:21–1:22:57"), (4979, "1:22:59–1:23:55"), (5035, "1:23:55–1:25:12"), (5115, "1:25:15–1:26:19"), (5193, "1:26:33–1:27:32"), (5252, "1:27:32–1:28:44"), (5327, "1:28:47–1:29:38"), (5428, "1:30:28–1:31:31"), (5491, "1:31:31–1:31:53"), (5522, "1:32:02–1:32:37"), (5564, "1:32:44–1:33:54"), (5639, "1:33:59–1:35:04"), (5725, "1:35:25–1:36:39"), (5800, "1:36:40–1:37:38"), (5858, "1:37:38–1:38:36"), (5916, "1:38:36–1:39:31"), (5971, "1:39:31–1:40:39"), (6102, "1:41:42–1:42:24"), (6144, "1:42:24–1:43:00"), (6180, "1:43:00–1:43:36"), (6218, "1:43:38–1:44:12"), (6255, "1:44:15–1:44:53"), (6293, "1:44:53–1:45:42"), (6362, "1:46:02–1:47:02"), (6422, "1:47:02–1:47:30"), (6451, "1:47:31–1:48:09"), (6503, "1:48:23–1:50:21"), (6623, "1:50:23–1:50:58"), (6674, "1:51:14–1:52:43"),), id: 499)

=== 7.9 Attributes of essence and action; divine immutability
// NODE P02.C01.S09
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML244:172-191 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("There is a difference between the \"Howness\" (Howness in the actions of God) and the identity of what God is. These are two separate things. This is something we have as Muslims: in theology, in creed, we distinguish between the Attributes of Action and the Attributes of Essence.", "What Christians often do is that they try to say, we don't understand how God operates in action (which we would agree, because we have not seen God; we have not seen the majesty of God). But what God is, is a completely different thing. You cannot use the same idea of \"how he does something\" and apply it to \"what God is\" to begin with.", "So when we're talking about what God is: All-Knowing, we all understand what that means; God is All-Powerful, we all understand what that means. We understand these attributes of the essence: God is not subject to death; God is eternal, with no beginning, no end. These attributes of essence we understand as human beings, that God communicated to us, because they are within the realm of our understanding as humans.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Catholic Grandfather Examines The Teachings Of Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tyYeayKMNwM", ((507, "8:27–8:40"), (526, "8:46–9:05"), (549, "9:09–9:22"),), id: 500)
// U ML383:422-490 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("What God is, is His essential Attributes. What God does can be this or can be that. I'm talking about the essential, what makes God God by definition. The minute you confuse what God is with what God does, you don't have a god anymore, because in order for me to use the word God, the term God, I need to already have an idea, certain attributes, certain definition of what God is. And what God does is predicated on what God is. Meaning: if God was not all-powerful \"is\", he could not do whatever he wants.", "So because God is All-Knowing, he can know the future; because God is All-Knowing, he can act in a specific way. But we're talking about what God is right now: God is always All-Knowing. And when you say God does whatever He wants: He can do whatever He wants, but He doesn't do anything, because he's All-knowing. For example, God would not do Injustice to you; He would not put you in Hellfire without any reason. He can, but He won't. It's like me saying to you, an analogy: you can beat up a baby, but you would never do that, because you're a human, you have human attributes, a compassionate person. So what God is determines what God does.", "So God is All-Powerful, All-Knowing; He does not become ignorant at any point of time, because if He did become ignorant, He's no longer God. God defines Himself in the Bible: He says He's All-Knowing. So it's not me, it's the Bible. For example, God is Loving and Just; therefore He would do loving things and He wouldn't do unjust things.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Australian Christians Challenge Muslim On Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H2KKhpxS26M", ((1213, "20:13–20:46"), (1257, "20:57–21:31"), (1294, "21:34–22:21"),), id: 501)
// U ML168:1207-1281 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Actualizing attributes is not the same as possessing attributes. You can possess an attribute and not actualize it, no problem; you don't need to actualize the attribute to possess it. All of His attributes are Eternal; not that all His attributes are actualized. His attributes are bound by His will: if He wills to actualize His attributes, exercise them, He exercises them. Some attributes are attributes of the Essence, which means they're always active: for instance, Knowledge, Power. These are essential attributes, and then there are attributes of actions, like creating, loving, these things. These attributes can be actualized based on God's will: whenever He wills to actualize them, He will actualize them.", "\"God just is\": that makes no sense; it just means God is God. What do you mean, God does not change? Change from what aspect? Did God, at some point, choose to create you? So, before that He didn't choose to create you. And after He created you, He observes you. He did not observe you before creating you, because you didn't exist. If you call that \"Change\", then your definition of change is problematic. It depends on what you mean by \"Change\".", "What doesn't change, for example: God's attribute does not change, from what sense? Meaning that God is All-Powerful and He'll become weak one day, or He's All-Knowing and one day He's not going to be knowing: these things, no! These essential attributes of God will always be present within God, Eternal and Perfect, maximally Perfect.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Attempts To Outsmart A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dSte7AZ7Rpc", ((2636, "43:56–44:45"), (2708, "45:08–46:09"), (2771, "46:11–46:23"),), id: 502)
// U ML219:1254-1284 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("I believe God does that because God knows that this person deserves it. He deserves that eternal hellfire; it's not just if he doesn't deserve it. God is not a human. He's not like, \"Okay, this is very difficult for me to do, but I'm going to do it.\" The creator is the all wise, all knowing, all powerful. Whatever the creator does stems from wisdom and stems from justice.", "Anger is there, but it's not the anger of humans. Allah says in the Quran, there's nothing like unto Allah. So the anger of boiling blood that we have as humans, that's not the anger of the creator. So whenever we use a description of Allah's attributes, that is not like the attributes that humans possess; it is unlike it in every way possible. That's our belief as Muslims. That's why we say God is not a man. But not only that, the Bible literally word for word says God is not a man, in Hosea chapter 9 verse 11 and in Numbers 23 17, I believe.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Lantern | Arab | Sneak0 | Young Don | Warner | Discuss Religion”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XCqP_UWikkY", ((3835, "1:03:55–1:04:53"), (3895, "1:04:55–1:05:25"),), id: 503)
// U ML410:558-615 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("\"Will\" and \"biological\" attribute? Will has something to do with biology? Gravity is a law which is in work within the universe, something that we can observe. What we call gravity is us explaining something that is already in existence. It's just a concept. So you cannot say, \"Will does X, Y and Z or doesn't do X, Y and Z,\" because it's just a concept that we give to something which is present in reality. If you accept that gravity is a law, you need to explain who the lawmaker is. Why is that law in existence? And that comes back to the point that I was making, that \"that thing\" decided that the law will be in this way. And if it decides that the law would be in this way, that's what we refer to as a \"will\".", "Now, when I say to you \"will\" and you say to me that that's a human concept, that doesn't make any sense, because the words we're using are all human words in order for us to explain something. So any word I would use would be a human concept in the end.", "And we do not say that the Creator does not possess things that we possess, but on a spectrum, on a level which is infinite. I have mercy, the Creator has ultimate Mercy. I have love, the Creator has ultimate Love. I have a will, the Creator has an unrestricted Will. I can only have a will in a paradigm which I'm restricted to; the Creator can will whatever He wants to will. His Will is enforceable on anything and everything. Nothing can force upon Him to choose anything. So the Creator has a will, but it's not similar to my will. And there is no problem with the fact that the Creator has a Will and I have a will.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Questions The Quran! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P_3UFyI5Xm0", ((1288, "21:28–23:08"), (1392, "23:12–23:21"), (1406, "23:26–23:55"),), id: 504)
// U ML338:316-366 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("First: Allah existed before the universe. When Allah created the universe, he put Creation with it. Allah is not in the universe, according to our belief. So I create a universe, and that universe is step by step; I'm putting creation within it. What's the problem? Why does there need to be someone else with Allah? You have to give me a reason for why is the problem for God to be alone? Your argument needs reasons!", "There's a position in Islam that Allah was eternally creating. So there was always creation, but that creation is not as Eternal as Allah! Why is it not as Eternal as Allah? Because a reaction has to be preceded by an action, and the action has to be preceded by the individual.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Preacher Attempts To Evangelise Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-C2GyGeecbo", ((876, "14:36–15:15"), (927, "15:27–15:42"),), id: 505)
// U ML257:97-148 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Allah: this is the Creator! The Creator is All-Powerful. You as a believer in God: the God that you believe in cannot create from nothing, you're saying? He's weak? Did God tell you, \"Before I create, every time, I would need something\"?", "Also, you're asking from an Islamic perspective. The Quran is explicit about this. The Quran (Annahl16:40) says that (if Allahﷻ wants to create something, He says, \"Be!\" and it is). Allah says: أَن نَقُولَ لَهُۥ كُن فَيَكُونُ (that \"We say to it كُن, \"Be\" فَيَكُونُ, and it is\"). It's as simple as that for The Creator. Allahﷻ is an All-Powerful God; He has All-Powerful Attributes, and therefore all Allahﷻ does is to say \"Be!\" and it is, and that thing then comes into existence, just like how everything else comes into existence.", "There's no requirement of something else for God to create, or then nothing can exist to begin with, because at the starting point things have to come from non-existence; things have to be created from nothing by definition. So there are times where God creates from nothing, even under your worldview, whichever worldview that is, if you believe God created the world. And there was no world to be formed or formless.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Hidden Christian Attempts To Trap Muslim But This Happens! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fPij4N3oke4", ((334, "5:34–6:37"), (400, "6:40–7:17"), (441, "7:21–7:47"),), id: 506)
