#passage(("Allah in the Quran says: and he is the most forgiving, the most loving. This is what we believe about Allah. Allah is the most loving, without a shadow of a doubt. But just like Allah is loving and merciful, He's also just. So being loving doesn't mean now that you will forgive the wrongdoers, or you will not leave the people who are doing evil and give justice to the weak. That's not love. Love comes with justice when it comes to Islam. So we have a balance in Islam. It's not just the idea of love and it's not just the idea of deeds or punishment; it's a balance.", "Allah is loving, and that's why you see the love of Allah in the commands of Allah. You see the mercy of Allah in the commands of Allah. For example, Allah tells us that if we do one good deed, it's multiplied automatically by 10. So I give charity, now it's multiplied by 10. Allah says up to 700, and Allah increases to whom he wills. So you could give one pound, $1, whatever, and you can get millions of good deeds for that, like the person who donated a million dollar, depending on your intention. Because I could be poor and I only have a dollar and I donated everything I have; someone else is a millionaire and he donates a dollar, it's nothing.", "So God will reward you based upon your intentions. If you do one bad deed, it's one bad deed. If I want to do a bad deed and then I stop, I get a good deed. I didn't do anything good; all I did is, I was about to do something bad and I stopped. If you do good deeds, Allah uses the good deeds to forgive your bad deeds. Allah says the good deeds, they remove the bad deeds. Allah says if you avoid the major sins, we forgive your minor sins.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady Grills Muslim With Questions Then A Surprise Happens! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JOpckbnPmsM", ((1670, "27:50–28:31"), (1715, "28:35–29:16"), (1757, "29:17–30:04"),), id: 508)
// U ML245:278-287 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("It doesn't say God is love. God is loving: that's very different. One of Allah's names is [Al-Wadud], means excessively loving. So Allah, the Creator we believe in, is a balanced creator. He's not a creator that just: hit me one cheek, I'll give you the other cheek. We don't have this stuff. We have Justice: you do good, you get good; you do bad, you get bad. We defend ourselves, we protect ourselves. Some people, if you're nice to them, they end up transgressing even further; they need a level of restraint.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Pantheist Challenges The Muslim View Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m4s4GlDTg28", ((1386, "23:06–23:44"),), id: 509)
// U ML171:37-94 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("We don't believe Allah is more Merciful than Just. We believe all of the Attributes of Allah are MAXIMALLY PERFECT. When it comes to Allah's Anger and Allah's Mercy, now there is a relationship there, in which Allah says His Mercy precedes His Anger. But \"Just\" is a completely different concept. Allah is completely Just; He applies His justice with everyone; nobody will ever encounter any injustice from Allah. So Allah can give a favour to someone, due to His Mercy; He can make His favour upon someone. But NEVER would Allah be unjust to anyone in any circumstantial situation. Allah is clear in the Quran (anNisa4:40). Allah says: إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَا يَظْلِمُ مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍۢ ۖ (Allah does not do injustice the weight of an atom).", "Also, in a Narration, the Prophet has informed us that \"Allah has made injustice forbidden upon himself\". Then Allah said to us: \"we shouldn't be doing injustice between one another\". So injustice is NOT a possibility; it's impossible when it comes to the Creator, when it comes to Allahﷻ.", "When I said that, that's not me really saying it; that's the text. You like it, which is good; that means you like what the Revelation says in Islam. But in contrast, you might like a concept and say: I agree with it, but that does not mean that it's present in your text. When I'm presenting things, I would present them based on text, because it's not based on my opinion.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Mormon Debates Muslim On The Validity Of His Faith! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0dXtlG11q-g", ((206, "3:26–4:08"), (253, "4:13–4:31"), (319, "5:19–5:42"),), id: 510)
