#passage(("I wouldn't use that terminology. It's not \"God tries to do His best\"; it's more of a human kind of thing. We're very specific about terminology when we speak about God. You'll get offended if someone misrepresents you; the Creator of the Universe, we're very clear about the words that we use about Him.", "You're saying that Allah, His intention is not to punish. Allahﷻ says in the Quran (anNisa4:147): مَّا يَفْعَلُ ٱللَّهُ بِعَذَابِكُمْ (What would Allah do with your punishment?) If you believe and you're thankful, what would Allahﷻ do with your punishment? Allahﷻ is not trying to punish the people, essentially.", "He's also Just. He's not going to make equal a person who didn't, for example, drink all his life with someone who did drink. Also, repentance is there, and repentance and forgiveness before you die: that's also something that is there and open, the door that Allahﷻ keeps open. Let's just say someone doesn't repent and he does this and he dies upon it. It's Allah's judgment and justice to say that he cannot be equal to the person who did it. That person stopped himself, he obeyed My command, and he struggled, but he still obeyed. You didn't obey, and you did fulfill your desires and did what you wanted to do. How can Allah make them equal?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Enquires About Qur'an Contradictions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0shFTfDDiro", ((908, "15:08–15:25"), (932, "15:32–15:42"), (994, "16:34–17:19"),), id: 511)
