#passage(("Nothing from what you've presented has to do with logic; it's more emotional. You're just saying, emotionally, to you, you don't \"feel\" that it's necessary for that Creator to demand recognition, or whatever term you want to use to describe what you are saying. But in the end, Allah never says that He needs this. Allah explicitly says the opposite in the Quran (Yunus10:99) (If God wanted everyone to worship Him, He can make everyone worship Him): an All-Powerful Creator Who has already created angels that worship Him 24/7, as Allah says in the Quran (alAnbiya21:26-27): بَلْ عِبَادٌ مُّكْرَمُونَ (Rather they're righteous, honourable servants) (that they don't precede Allah in speech, and they do everything that Allah commands them to do) يُسَبِّحُونَ اللَّيْلَ وَالنَّهَارَ لَا يَفْتُرُونَ (Day and night, they glorify God, they never stop). This Creation already exists (Alanbiya21:20).", "But there's a difference between Him being worthy of it, and it being something that is common sense, that you need to show gratitude to the One Who's given you everything that you have, and at the same time, the way you're trying to portray it, where God is like: 'Oh, I want everyone to worship Me!' Allah says in the Quran (azZumar39:7): فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ عَنكُمْ (Allah is free of you). He doesn't need you. But He doesn't approve disbelief for His servants; He doesn't want you to be a disbeliever (azZumar39:7). It's not good for you to be a disbeliever.", "Of course, He is independent. That's what the word الصمد (asSamad) means. That's one of the essential Attributes of God: God is independent of need. He doesn't need recognition, or whatever term you want to use, from anyone. On the other hand, He deserves recognition.", "Now, what do we mean by that? Let's give an example of one's family. Let's say you're born; your parents took care of you as a kid, they changed your diapers, they fed you, taught you, took you to schools; your father was working day and night, 