#passage(("There is major and minor. Do you know who gave the definition? Prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam. So not looking for any opinions, because the prophet already gave us the definition. And we have also minor. First, it is whatever the [Shar'] calls [shirk] or something similar to it, or all of these phrases which are in the same meaning, and it leads you to major.", "An example of that, because the example things becomes clear: the prophet, he said whoever hangs or holds an amulet and he believes this amulet gives him protection, something that gives him actual protection, it's protecting him, then he's committed [shirk]. So that is minor, that leads you to major, that you worship now other than Allah. And the prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam, he also said: whoever makes an oath with other than Allah, he's committed [shirk], because you are holding that thing in a position of Allah. In a way you're beginning to do that: you're making an oath with other than Allah, so you're beginning to make that thing equal with Allah, which is what we said is major. You might not believe it; someone says \"my mother this and this and that\"; you don't believe your mother is equal with Allah, but you use that term, and it can lead you to major.", "Minor [shirk] can be apparent, like the actions that we're saying, outward, or also can be inward, like showing off: you're doing an action so people can praise you; you're not doing it for Allah. You're standing, you're praying, you're beautifying your voice in the most beautiful way so people can praise you and say, look at this great reciter, not because you are doing it for the sake of Allah as an action of worship. Because the actions of worship has to be purely for Allah. So when you associate with Allah a people or anything else, or fame or anything else other than Allah, then that is considered Association.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”"