#passage(("Our position is the only person you can call upon is Allah. I'm not going to even use the term \"Sunnis\", because the Muslims are those who follow Quran. So we look at the Quran. Allahﷻ says in the Quran (alBayyina98:5) وَمَاۤ أُمِرُوۤا۟ إِلَّا لِیَعۡبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ مُخۡلِصِینَ لَهُ ٱلدِّینَ: They were only commanded to worship Allahﷻ ALONE, not to associate anyone with Him. Then Allahﷻ says (Ala'raf7:194) إِنَّ الَّذِينَ تَدْعُونَ مِن دُونِ اللَّهِ (those who you call upon other than Allah) عِبَادٌ أَمْثَالُكُمْ (They are servants like you) فَادْعُوهُمْ فَلْيَسْتَجِيبُوا لَكُمْ إِن كُنتُمْ صَادِقِينَ (Call upon them and let them help you if youre truthful). Do they have Legs they're walking? Or do they have hands they're using? (Ala'raf7:195) They're not alive, they're dead, those people you're calling upon other than Allah! Then Allahﷻ also says in the Quran (Alahqaf46:5) وَمَنْ أَضَلُّ مِمَّن يَدْعُو مِن دُونِ اللَّهِ (Who's more misguided than the one who calls other than Allah). Anyone other than Allah. مِن دُونِ اللَّهِ means other than Allah. مَن لَّا يَسْتَجِيبُ لَهُ إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ الْقِيَامَةِ (They're not going to respond to you until the Day of Judgment).", "Shi'as say that they don't call upon others. They say they call upon using their high status in front of the eyes of Allah. Do they say \"Ya Ali\" (Ô Ali!)? Or say \"Ya Hussain\"? You go to Hussainiyat and hear them say Ya Ali 24h, 24/7. So the fact is, we're saying they wouldn't say you cannot do this, even if you said they're not doing it. Majority of them, many of them doing it. Maybe the ones around you don't, but they say you can do it. So if they all agree and say this is an association (Shirk), then we don't need to discuss this. We're only discussing it because it exists for them.", "And Allahﷻ says, to finish the verse (who is more misguided than who calls other than Allah), (And they are unaware of you calling upon them) وَإِذَا حُشِرَ النَّاسُ كَانُوا لَهُمْ أَعْدَاءً (Alahqaf46:6) (And when Allah resurrects the people and gathers them, they will be your enemy). وَكَانُوا بِعِبَادَتِهِمْ كَافِرِينَ (And they will disavow in your worship). So Allah called \"calling upon someone\" worship.", "Now you're giving me examples: go to the doctor, go to a friend.. I'm giving you quranic verses. The Shi'a can go up and down, bring me all of that.. I'm not interested in what they have to say. I'm interested in what my Lord said to me, that they claim they believe in. Allah says to the Prophetﷺ: (Annaml27: 80) إِنَّكَ لَا تُسْمِعُ الْمَوْتَىٰ (You cannot make the dead hear). Allah explicitly mentions that in the Quran, that they don't hear. There's an exception where Allah made the people hear one time with the Prophetﷺ; that doesn't mean that this happens regularly. We're not talking about exceptions to the rule. We're talking generally that people don't hear.", "What is the problematic thing about calling upon someone who's dead? The problematic thing is this, is that you say that that person, whoever they are, can hear all voices at the same time, because all of us are calling upon them at the same time, can understand all languages, because all of us are speaking different languages. Even the Arabic that we're using today is not the same Arabic that 'Ali was using. You're saying that person who is dead never dies, which is a characteristic of Allah. And they don't get mixed up, which is only a characteristic of Allah.", "What does knowledge have to do with what we're saying? But that's still not enough to hear all voices at the same time and understand all languages. And where does Allah say He did that to them? It's all hypothetical scenarios and examples and games, because they know the Quran is against them. I asked you for one verse. I'll give you tens of verses for what I say. Because what we believe as Muslims is found in the Quran, in our Book, of course. The creeds that we believe have to be in our Book.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Fully Dismantles The Shia Position! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t7vhKiTrvNA", ((1457, "24:17–25:25"), (1532, "25:32–26:24"), (1587, "26:27–27:13"), (1635, "27:15–28:04"), (1689, "28:09–28:53"), (1752, "29:12–29:59"),), id: 567)
