=== 9.3 Sources of creed and the understanding of the Salaf
// NODE P02.C03.S03
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML239:270-497 | The Muslim Lantern | 9
#passage(("Where do we derive our sources of knowledge when it comes to religion? So we have four things. We have Quran; we have the Sunnah of the Prophet, his life, Etc.; we have the consensus, the agreement of either all scholars in a specific day and age, or the companions of the Prophet, on a matter; and the fourth thing is analogy. We're not interested in what you think; this analogy is based on the Quran and Sunnah.", "Now, with whose understanding, this Quran and Sunnah? This brother here, from a specific country; his brother, he is from another country; his way of thinking is based on how he was raised; your way of thinking, my way of thinking. Should we listen to their explanation of the Quran and Sunnah?", "Today there's a Calamity, a huge Calamity. That Calamity is: everyone opens Tik Tok Channel, this channel, that channel: \"I read this verse yesterday, and this verse actually means this\", and he doesn't know how to tie his shoes, but he's going to tell you what the Quran means. So we have This calamity today of everyone bringing his own understanding, people rejecting completely the prophet, saying: \"I will understand this Quran; Prophet is just bringing me mail; I'm going to interpret the mail after I got the mail.\" We have these people, and there's not one or two; there's many people like this.", "So whose understanding do we take when it comes to the Quran and Sunnah? Because now, when you speak to your friend and you say \"Allah says\": \"Oh, but my uncle said this, but my nephew said…\" We don't care about your nephew and uncle. We get it not just from the sahaba, but from the righteous predecessors. They are the companions of the Prophet, their students, and their students.", "Why do we get it from these people? The reason we get it from these people is multiple things. First thing is from the Quran. Allah explicitly says in the Quran the description of those Companions of the Prophet. Allah says: Muhammad is the messenger of Allah; those who are with him, they are harsh, strong against disbelievers, merciful with one another; you see them prostrating, worshiping Allah. Allah says he's pleased with them in multiple different locations. Allah says that he promised them Jenna. Where are we trying to go? Jenna. So if there is someone who's going to Jenna, I should imitate what he's doing, because he's doing the right thing clearly.", "Allah says: why are you not spending for the sake of Allah, and for Allah is the inheritance of the heavens and the Earth? Those who spent and fought before the conquest of Mecca are not equal to those who did after, because it was more difficult: Islam was few in number; it was more difficult to fight that time; after the conquest there was more strength, so it was easier to fight. Then Allah said: and all of them Allah promised the best thing: Jenna. So Allah is saying he promised these companions; he told us he's pleased with them, that he's praised them, and they're praised in the [Torah] and the [Injil]. Is it just that Allah is pleased with them, and their praise, and all? No, but Allah explicitly told us to follow them in verse 100: and the first [forerunners], those who migrated to Medina to fight with the prophet, and those who supported the prophet when he came to Medina, these tribes that were there that supported the prophet when he left Mecca and came to Medina: they're all the companions of the Prophet. Then Allah says: and those who follow them in goodness and righteousness. This is what we're trying to be; this is what we're trying to do.", "So the students, we call them \"those who followed them in righteousness\". So Allah says: those who follow them in righteousness, Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with him; Allah has prepared for them Gardens where rivers flow beneath. So this we take from the Quran: we follow the [companions].", "From the Sunnah also, from the Hadith: the prophet, he said: the best of generations are my generation, then the one who come after it, then the one who comes after it. These are the best generations with the testimony of the prophet in the faith. That means they are the example for us to learn from, because they are the best of generations. Prophet said: upon you is my tradition and the tradition of the companions of the prophet, [Abu Bakr], and all of these people. So the evidences are many, many.", "And just using Common Sense: those people were the students, the direct students of the Prophet. So who is best to learn about the prophet and about religion from? The people who are direct students, seeing how the prophet is behaving, what the prophet is saying, what the prophet is doing. Not only that, they were there when the verses was revealed. Were You There When the Quran was revealed, any of us? Did you hear the Quran from the prophet? No. These people heard it directly from the prophet, took it from him, and they were there where the Quran was descending. In fact, the Quran descended about some of them. There are verses in the Quran that directly speaks about the companions, like [Abu Bakr] being with the prophet; Allah is mentioning him as a companion in the Quran. Companions are mentioned with name, because the Quran was even revealed about them. So if you just want to use even the common sense, you'll know that these are the people you learn from.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ULnwXbVf_cM", ((650, "10:50–11:38"), (706, "11:46–12:04"), (745, "12:25–13:05"), (787, "13:07–13:35"), (815, "13:35–14:44"), (884, "14:44–16:23"), (983, "16:23–17:08"), (1028, "17:08–17:38"), (1061, "17:41–18:50"),), id: 600)
// U ML145:563-594 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("Right now, what you're trying to do is to make Islam similar to the subjective worldview that you're following, but it's absolutely NOT. And the reason is: Islam has a definition. The definition of Islam is whatever is in the Quran -which is the Words of God- and the teachings of the prophet Muhammad. I told you, what they believe, ask them: where is it in the text? And you will end up realising that it doesn't exist anywhere in text. Therefore, it's NOT an interpretation, and it's NOT Islam. It is just someone who came up with an idea that has nothing to do with the religion. Islam, unlike Liberalism or any other subjective ideology, has clear bounds and definitions. You cannot come up with something and just say, \"I'm a Muslim, therefore it's a part of Islam\". No! Allah says in the Quran, explicitly, in Chapter:4 (anNisa4:59): (If you disagree over anything), ANYTHING, (return it to the Quran and the Messenger).", "Anything that any Muslim can come up with, any group, any sect, any belief, whatever: if they tell me \"do this\", our first question as Muslim is: where is that found in the Quran and in the prophetic tradition? If the answer is \"it doesn't\", then you're a human being telling me to do something, and I don't care what you say. I follow God; I don't follow you or any other human being. So until you present the text, because that's what I follow, your opinion is irrelevant. So, those people, it's either that there was a miscommunication, and you misunderstood what they believe, or they do believe that, and they're not Muslims.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Liberal Woman Debates Muslim On The Need For Religion! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kBJxu5UYcy4", ((1820, "30:20–31:07"), (1874, "31:14–31:45"),), id: 601)
