#passage(("This isn't me kicking people out of the fold, or anything like that, but these are definitely things that people need to keep in mind if they want to follow the prophet, peace be upon them. If you want to be true to yourself, if you want to be someone who is upon what he was upon, then you have to do this: you have to accept what he has taught without putting conditions, unreasonable conditions.", "So, for example, there are people that came and said: \"I only accept narrations of [aqidah] that are [narrated] by [tawatur].\" So now this is subjective issue, because you ask different people, \"How many are you referring to?\", so they'll say, \"I'm referring to 10.\" So if it's nine people: \"No, I can't accept nine people writing a Hadith from the prophet; it has to be ten.\" That's a silly condition that some people have made in order to reject the reports.", "And that's what [Ahlul] Hadith were famous for doing: adhering to the statements of the Prophet, peace upon him, adhering to what they find in the Quran, not interpreting away, manipulating, rendering the words of Allah and the messenger, peace be upon him, to be meaningless, void of wisdom. I say: what's the wisdom of Allah saying this? What's the wisdom of the Prophet, peace upon him, saying this, if in reality everything is supposed to be interpreted away to fit your ideology, to fit your position, if it's to fix something that you've believed in previously? That's the issue.",), "Farid Responds, “The Hadith of the 73 Sects - 2nd AIM Conference (2022)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nE0bMMLxFYw", ((1771, "29:31–29:59"), (1802, "30:02–30:52"), (1864, "31:04–31:48"),), id: 603)
// U FR134:821-929 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("I'd like to share my thoughts on the qualities of the people of Hadith, the people that are upon what the prophet, peace upon him, and his companions were upon. The people of Hadith, they shared some qualities that sometimes we'll find in other groups, but these qualities shine through them.", "The first, perhaps most important quality is that they preserved the Hadith, and they preserve the statements of the companions. And this in itself shows that they are the people of Hadith, and that they are the people that were closest to the prophet, peace be upon him. It's a natural assumption: would you think that the [Mu'tazila] would be from Hadith? There are barely anyone that were known for compiling a Hadith; very, very small number. When you find the [Mu'tazili] who was known for writing down Hadith and for compiling Hadith and that sort of thing, they either have really small works, or it's mentioned in their biographies: \"he was a [Mu'tazili] that cared about [Hadith]\"; it's very rare. And you know, just by that alone, that they did not care as much as the people of Hadith cared about following the prophet, peace be upon him. A lot of the times they were known for other things: they were known for being very well-versed in a Greek philosophy and things of that nature. You can just tell by the works that they've authored.", "Another aspect is the people Hadith were the most knowledgeable [in] what the prophet, peace be upon him, said and what he did not say. This is very important: being aware of what he said and what he did not say gives one the ability to determine what he was upon. Of course, I'm specifically referring to the authentication of reports and the weakening of reports. Some people have no idea how to deal with this; they don't know what he said. Some of them have compilations; they assume everything in the compilation is authentic, because they do not have a [rijal] system. At the same time, you have others that absolutely reject everything that's attribute to him, for example the Hadith [rejecter] movements, which is relatively modern, barely existed in the early centuries. And the ability and the care, when it came to grading [reports], that itself is just clear-cut proof that they cared more about what rasulullah was upon than anyone else, and that's why people had to go to them in order to determine whether the prophet, peace be upon Him, gave this ruling or not.", "Number three: their foundations were limited, and the foundations were mainly limited to the Quran, Hadith; that's mainly it. They didn't have too many foundations that caused confusion, that allowed more opinions, that's allowed a larger variety of positions. No, they mainly stuck with Quran, Hadith, and those limitations that they imposed upon themselves caused them to be closer to rasulullah than other groups.",), "Farid Responds, “The Hadith of the 73 Sects - 2nd AIM Conference (2022)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nE0bMMLxFYw", ((2219, "36:59–37:27"), (2247, "37:27–39:14"), (2356, "39:16–40:46"), (2449, "40:49–41:28"),), id: 604)

=== 9.4 The Jahmiyyah, Mu'tazila, Ash'aris and Maturidis
// NODE P02.C03.S04
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML168:1127-1167 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("We don't say the Quran is eternal; depends on what you mean by that. I'm a Muslim. My 'Aqeeda source is from the Prophetic Teachings, from the Quran, from what the Companions believed, that the Prophetﷺ told them to believe. That's my Creed; that should be the case for every Muslim.", "Do you know the five principles of the Mu'tazilah? Do you know the names of who the Mu'tazilah were, the founders? If you just heard a name, you cannot ask me if I'm something when you don't know what that thing is. No Muslim would believe the Quran is created.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Attempts To Outsmart A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dSte7AZ7Rpc", ((2461, "41:01–41:19"), (2485, "41:25–42:20"),), id: 605)
// U ML239:2870-3175 | The Muslim Lantern | 10
#passage(("Some of the groups: the head of the groups, one of the earliest of the groups, if not the earliest of them all: the [Jahmiyyah]. These were people in the early generation. However, when Islam spread, Muslims started dealing with non-muslims. In the beginning it was just the Arabian Peninsula, Arabs dealing with Arabs. Then they started dealing with the Greeks, and translating the books of the philosophers, and encountering Aristotle writings, and Socrates, and all of these people. And what ended up happening is some of the Muslims, they were debating some of these people of philosophy. And when they debate these people of philosophy, sometimes they don't want to say \"we believe in it because it's in the Quran\"; they want to use the intellect and put the intellect over the text.", "Now, if there is any disease in what we're about to talk about, it comes back to this issue: you do not want to stick to the Quran and the Sunnah, and you want to use something else, which will always be based on your desires and the intellect, always.", "Now, when they started reading the books of the philosophers, they starting adopting their principles, because they want to win the debate. You see people today debating; what happens in debate: sometimes winning the debate is more important. So because they want to win the debate, they will compromise the principles of Islam. Now, they wanted to prove something basic: it's an Evidence to show that this world has a beginning. So what they ended up doing: because Allah has names and attributes, and the philosophers told them, \"if there's names and attributes, you have a problem: many gods\", so they ended up denying all Allah's names and attributes. No names, no attributes.", "They say Allah did not speak to [Musa]. In the verse, Allah is putting emphasis that Allah spoke to [Musa]. And we believe Allah speaks. And when we say someone speaks, it means with sounds, the letters. We all know this is how people speak; when we say \"speaks\", this is what speech is. Now, in relation to Allah, we said we have something in similar, the meaning, but Allah's sound is not like our sound. And in the Hadith there's an example for that, where Allah speaks: the person who's far away, he hears him the same way the person who's close by hears him. Is my sound like this? Is any creation sound like this? No. The speech of Allah is not like ours, but Allah speaks with sounds and letters.", "These people now, they said: the sounds, letters, beginning, end: that doesn't make any sense to my mind, so we need to deny all of this. And that's why they were excommunicated by all of the righteous predecessors. Even the misguided groups today, they would be upset if you call them [Jahmiyyah], because we have clear statements from the righteous predecessors saying that they are disbelievers. No one calls himself that name in this day and age.", "But there's a reason I'm mentioning it: because, just like we as human beings have children and grandchildren and progeny, the [Jahmiyyah] have also children, and grandchildren, who took from their principles, but they disagreed with them. Just like your son does not follow everything you tell him: he takes many things from you, but then he deviates a little bit; and then his son takes from him, and then he deviates a little bit. But they're all in this case problematic, because the father here is the source of all misguidance.", "So the next Children of the [Jahmiyyah] came: they are the [Mu'tazila]. They said: \"These are very bad; they deny Allah's attributes. We're not going to do that. We affirm that Allah has names, but no attributes.\" Allah is the most powerful, but he has no power; the most wise, but he has no wisdom. Do you understand language? He would say to you: \"Yes, the reason we do that is because when we debated with the philosophers, same issue: they told us that more than one attribute means more than one God, because each of these attributes is eternal, and therefore you have more than one Eternal, and therefore you have more than one God.\" We say: but are you separating the attributes from the individual? If I speak to any of you, and I say your speech is another person, and your hearing is another person: where is this intellect? But believe it or not, that was their principles.", "Then they ended up also bringing principles, principles of misguidance. But I'll tell you something very important: the people of falsehood always do this: they name misguidance with beautiful names, because if they tell you a bad name, no one is going to be interested. So they tell you: we have five main principles. The first one: to deny Allah's attributes. Now, take all of the attributes: we say just one Allah. So they call [it tawhid]; so they beautify it with a beautiful name to misguide the people. And trust me, this is a principle you'll always see the people using. So they will tell you \"we follow the righteous [predecessors]\"; they will tell you these things. But when you start listening to them, do they quote the Hadith, the Sunnah? No. Do they quote the companions? Did he quote the Quran? No. He'll tell you: my intellect, our teacher, this person said this, that person said.", "The second principle they had, it's called Justice. What do they mean by Justice? They say it's not just if Allah knows, or Allah wills, and we're punished. We Believe Allah wills and also have a will, but they say no, it's not just. So we have Justice. And then they have another principle, third principle: that principle they will call one state between two states. And what they mean by that is a person who commits a major sin is neither a Believer nor a disbeliever. So it's either you're a Believer or a disbeliever, while bringing another thing into the picture: they would say to you there's something in between. They were sitting down in the sitting, and then they said to [al-Hasan]: \"What do you say about the person who commits a major sin?\" He said to him: \"I see he's not a Believer, he's not a disbeliever; he's in a state between.\" And then he left the setting. \"He left us\": and that's where the term comes from; the name comes from this idea.", "What do they mean by applying the punishment that Allah say he's going to apply? Applying the punishment of Allah is that the people who commit sins, they're going to be in the fire forever. So if you're a Believer, as a Muslim, you're not going to leave the fire; you're going to remain in the fire for good. And they will say that is applying Allah's punishment: Allah said this punishment, and we're applying his punishment. So they use these names, and they try to change and manipulate and play games in order for them to deceive and confuse the masses.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ULnwXbVf_cM", ((6870, "1:54:30–1:55:38"), (6938, "1:55:38–1:55:59"), (6968, "1:56:08–1:56:53"), (7013, "1:56:53–1:57:49"), (7069, "1:57:49–1:58:49"), (7136, "1:58:56–1:59:27"), (7167, "1:59:27–2:00:58"), (7268, "2:01:08–2:02:47"), (7376, "2:02:56–2:04:44"), (7492, "2:04:52–2:05:37"),), id: 606)
// U ML312:263-324 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("It's not the Sufi School of thoughts. What he's saying is essentially the position of The Mu'tazila. Who did he mention? Wasil Ibn Ata'? Amr Ibn [Ubayd]? But the point is, he's referring to The Mu'tazila, which had an issue in Greek philosophy -which is completely ridiculous-, which is the idea that if God has multiple attributes, there is multiple gods, or if God creates something, there's multiple gods.", "The words of Allah is an attribute of Allah: the Kalam of Allah, the Speech of God, is an Attribute of God, just like your speech is your attribute. So it's the same issue. Just like if you compare it with Mercy, you see how ridiculous it is when you start comparing it with other Attributes. The problem is, he's confusing Christian theology now, of a Word becoming God, which is something we don't believe in to begin with. We believe the Attributes of Allah are always Eternal within Allahﷻ: means that Allah is all the time able to actualise these Attributes. He's all the time able to be Loving, Merciful, Create, Speak; these are eternal attributes within Allahﷻ. And then Allah Speaks! Now Allah creates and makes creation, and that does not mean therefore that there is other gods. How does that make any sense?", "These things are way above his understanding, because he doesn't know what he's talking about. So he read some things and got confused, and then he wrote them. That's why you're not going to find any Muslim today that believes that, or have an issue with Tawheed: \"Allahﷻ has many Attributes, therefore there's many gods!\"",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Why Do Muslims Reject The Claim Of Paul? Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HS9YyIgDMtg", ((827, "13:47–14:23"), (898, "14:58–15:34"), (953, "15:53–16:08"),), id: 607)
// U ML168:1170-1197 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("You have three things: you have a reciter, you have a recitation, and you have something that is being recited. The reciter is created; that which is being recited is the Words of God. These Words of God, God's attribute, His speech, is uncreated, because God is uncreated. So all His attributes, by definition, are the same. The created [human, etc.], his attributes are created, and the uncreated, His attributes are uncreated.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Attempts To Outsmart A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dSte7AZ7Rpc", ((2575, "42:55–43:29"),), id: 608)
// U ML239:3176-3496 | The Muslim Lantern | 12
#passage(("The grandchildren today are the biggest group now left. The grandfather and the father, come a time and they will die. So the grandchild that is left is [the Ash'aris]. Now, if you say that to [the Ash'aris], they say to [you]: \"What are you talking about? We are the people who refute!\" And that is actually true: they did refute, but by denying Allah's attributes as well, same thing, taking some and leaving some: \"We're going to refute these guys, but we're going to also bring our own intellectual principles, not Quran and Sunnah. We going to use our own intellect, and based on it we're going to affirm and deny.\"", "Now, if you ask, \"Do you affirm only seven names and attributes of Allah?\", he would say to you: \"No, you're a liar; [I affirm] many more attributes.\" So for us, we don't consider these actually attributes. That's why we say they only affirm seven, because whatever these other attributes they affirm, they're not in the Quran and Sunnah; they just made them up using intellectual principles, using their own intellect. They have these 20 so attributes that they're just made up. Seven of them are attributes we affirm: Life, Speech, sight, hearing, [will], knowledge, ability. So they affirm this, but actually, if you look in reality in their books, They Don't Really affirm them.", "So, for example, speech: they will say to you, \"[We affirm] speech for Allah.\" \"Does Allah speak with sound, letters?\" \"No.\" \"There's [a hadith]...\" \"No, no, you don't understand; come to me, I'll explain to you: speaks there does not mean speech, so sound there does not mean sound\", like language doesn't mean language. So they would, in this case, not really affirm the speech, because they're not saying that Allah speaks. What do they mean? They mean there's the Quran that we read. If you ask, \"Did Allah say?\", they say, \"What do you mean, say? There's something called the internal speech: Allah said it internally.\"", "Now, right now I've told you all something. Did you hear it? This, the internal speech: does anyone rational with his mind call this speech? Do we call this speech, the alien telecommunication I'm doing with you right now? It's not speech, is it? And the Arabs, none of them call this speech; doesn't exist. They say: Allah says, \"and they say within themselves, if only Allah would punish us with what we say\". So they say, like this, same way: Allah, he's got internal speech. We say to them: Allah said \"they say in themselves\"; he didn't say \"they say\"; he said \"they say in themselves\", to clarify clearly that this is a special thing here. So this verse is evidence against you, because there's no need to say \"in themselves\" if speech include both of them. And we say: Allah speaks; Allah calls upon. Can you call upon in your telecommunication? Allah says, \"and we called him\". So to call someone with a high voice: that cannot happen in any language, in Alien telecommunication.", "So they don't really affirm the speech of Allah, but they will pretend they do. So why? Because they have the same problem as their predecessors: they put the intellect above the text, and then they put fundamental principles, and use these fundamental principles in order for them to deny everything that goes against their own [principles].", "Their brothers: the grandson is not alone in this case; he's got a brother, one twin brother. That twin brother is called the [Maturidis]. They believe in very similar things, just like twins. There's very few disagreements with the twins, very small disagreement, but they're very close. So they increase one more attribute; they call [it takwin]. Where you bringing this from? Same thing: extra attributes from their pocket.", "Now, who is the head and the founder for these people? What did they tell you about the names of the people of the truth? People of the Sunnah, people of Hadith: is that a person? People who follow the righteous predecessors: is that one person? But you go to the people of falsehood: they're always like this: they had someone who innovated for them beliefs, and then they followed his principles, and they called after him. In this case, there's a person who was called Abu Hassan [al-Ash'ari], and he was born in 260 AH, and he died 324. And for the first 40 Years of his life he was like his father. He followed his father-in-law; it was actually his father-in-law. Then he started: \"It's time for puberty; we're not going to continue follow whatever we hear; we're going to disagree.\" So he started debating against his father-in-law, and then he went to another person called [Ibn Kullab], who's also misguided in his own way. And then he ended up, towards the end of his life, setting his own Creed.", "Now, unlike the other people, there's a happy ending for the story: he repented towards the end of his life. Many of the righteous predecessors did not accept his repentance — because it's not up to them to accept, to not; it's up to Allah — but meaning, apparently, they didn't bring him in the inner circles, because they were wary of him. All your life you move from one place to another place; you cannot just bring someone. We don't know what you're going to do next, so you give it some time. So this is not our opinion, that he repented: he wrote three books actually that show that he changed and repented. And he says, \"I'm on the same belief as Ahmed; now I'm repenting.\" And you have his students quoting these books, which means that they affirmed that he wrote these books; they're authentically attributed to him. But they don't follow him after he repented.", "Say to them: but this person said this over 300 years after the prophet. So the [Ummah], sleeping for the past 300 years, or what was going on? They'll say: \"No, the companions believe this; he just explained it.\" Start looking at his teachings: he doesn't quote the companions; nothing to do with what the companions were teaching, nothing to do with the Quran, nothing to do with the Hadith.", "They've got many whom they would call Scholars. They're called the people of [Kalam], because they talk much. They use the intellect too much, until all of the buildings fall, and then they talk, talk, talk, talk, but they don't reach a conclusion.", "They set principles. Any report from the prophet authentically attributed, we accept it. These people, on the other hand, you will see them telling you: \"No, if the report is not mass transmitted, then we can reject it.\" I would hope you wouldn't read, because it's not a good book for you to read, but you're welcome to read the writings of Razi, for example. Razi has a book: I believe in chapter either 31 or 32, he explains his principles: if any of these narrations, or any of the texts, opposes our intellect — he says intellect, but he means his intellect — then we can either change the apparent meaning or reject it. So they put these fundamental principles: the fundamental principle is the intellect precede the text, and we only accept them in Creed if they're Mass transmitted.", "So they go into misguidance not just in the names and attributes of Allah, but also in the reports; in most of the angles, they will have a disagreement.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ULnwXbVf_cM", ((7577, "2:06:17–2:07:27"), (7654, "2:07:34–2:08:46"), (7727, "2:08:47–2:09:35"), (7775, "2:09:35–2:11:20"), (7880, "2:11:20–2:11:50"), (7912, "2:11:52–2:12:35"), (7955, "2:12:35–2:14:31"), (8071, "2:14:31–2:15:37"), (8151, "2:15:51–2:16:11"), (8181, "2:16:21–2:16:43"), (8212, "2:16:52–2:18:22"), (8306, "2:18:26–2:18:37"),), id: 609)
