#passage(("I can be angry at God for something that happened. But the question is: did God promise me other than what happened to me? Did God promise me eternality, or eternality of my family members, or an easy life? Then does it make sense that I'm angry when God promised me the opposite? What God told me is what happened. But I'm still angry because I was more attached sometimes to people or things [than] I am attached to God, the one who created me and created those people, and who is able to unite me with them, and who's able to give them life, and who's the one who's given me life to begin with.", "So I think the best solution is for me to take that step, to follow and practice and bring God into my life, and then understand that this is how I'm going to be united with them in the afterlife. Cuz any day I'm not going to be here. I'm here today talking to you; tomorrow we could hear news I'm passed away. I know someone who one day just went to the hospital. It was not planned, and then they were told they have this cyst in their stomach that is huge, and they could die any day if it opens. Out of nowhere, you could one day realize that you have something that you're going to die the next day.", "Whether it's a medical thing, whether it's an accident, whether it's whatever, anyone can pass away. And this is what makes it very important that we take our decisions about believing in God and doing righteousness, and doing as much as we can as fast as we can; that we don't delay, that we don't just worry about distractions which are temporary. You want to hear music, you want to go out and party, you want this: this is what people are thinking about. All of it limited things, which doesn't really bring us happiness, brings us nothing but depression.", "People go into all of these wrong relationships, heartbroken, being used and abused; they built defenses. They have children out of wedlock, then they have to abort and kill an innocent human being. They drink, wake up in the morning completely gone, they want to throw up in the bathroom, they're regretting what they did. These are temporary things. They're distractions, but none of them are good for us, and they're not going to change the fact that we are going to die one day. Two things are certain: [taxes] and death.", "And I say always to people, it's not about age. I have family member that passed away so young, in their 20s, and in their baby years. Cuz a lot of people look at it this way: oh, average life expectancy. But who tells you you're going to be from the average?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “English Lady Asks Muslim Why God Allows Human Suffering! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwyXsinRrF8", ((330, "5:30–6:01"), (361, "6:01–6:39"), (405, "6:45–7:12"), (432, "7:12–7:56"), (479, "7:59–8:16"),), id: 647)
