#passage(("That's a separate thing. It's like whether the claim actually, it's attributed correctly to the source or not. That's a separate question. So is it true that that claim comes from the source, either creator or not? That's a question. And is it rational? Is the idea itself rational or not? That's a separate question.", "We were arguing the rationality of the principle. And I'm highlighting that the principle is not only very rational; in fact, it highlights the mercy of God. Some people bring that up and try to say that it highlights that God is not merciful. I say that's actually the opposite, because if God wasn't merciful, if he wanted, he could create you and put you in hellfire eternally, and that's it. That's done deal. He doesn't need to. You're a creation, and you have no power, no idea to say anything. But not only did he create you and give you a fair test — give you a test which is easy to succeed at.", "And all he said: just don't do this one thing. It's just one thing. Don't disbelieve in God or associate partners with God. And even if you become a Muslim, you can still fall and do sins and mistakes and whatever. You're not perfect. So I'm saying rationally, if you talk about the idea itself of the [eternal] hellfire being rational or irrational, I'm saying it's very rational.", "Do you know the difference between an internal critique and external critique? Cuz I think this is where the issue is coming. So you cannot look at the idea of eternal hellfire in Islam unless it's an internal critique, meaning that you would have to accept the full framework that Islam presents. Islam doesn't just present the idea of eternal hellfire in a vacuum. It also presents the definition of God, all of the things that I mentioned, the qualifications for reaching that level where you have eternal hellfire. So, it's not that Islam says you go to eternal hellfire and that's it. No, it says these are the qualifications. This is Islamic framework for the only way a