#passage(("How do we understand this verse? There's two ways to understand this verse of the Qur'an. The first way to understand this verse is that this is referring to the people who were Christians or Jews or people of the book and then accepted the Message of Islam, because the verse says: believe in Allah and do righteous deeds. And if you look at the Qur'an, there are many instances in the Qur'an where Allah speaks about the people of the book, and then say that if they hear the verses of the messenger, they believe (alMa'idah5:83). They hear the verses of the Qur'an, etc., they believe. Now they're Muslims. But he still called them the people of the book, because they were the people of the book before they accepted the Message of Islam.", "So one way to understand is that this verse is referring to those people, just like the end of chapter:3 of the Qur'an, last verse 199, mentions the same thing: that from the people of the book, those who believe; talks about them believing and doing righteous deeds. So they are no longer from the people of the book, they're Muslims, but they were. So Allah addresses them from that perspective. That's one understanding.", "Another understanding is that this is referring to the Christians or Jews or Sabians that were in the past, before Prophet Muhammadﷺ (peace be upon him) was sent. So it's referring to the Christians that were following Jesus, that believed in Allah, because they did believe in Allah, and they did do righteous deeds, and they followed their messenger. Those people will have no fear on the day of judgement, and they will go to paradise. We believe Jesus was a Muslim prophet. Moses was a Muslim prophet, meaning that they submitted their [will] to The One True God. That's what the word Muslim means, to submit your [will] to God. So we believe that whoever followed them and correctly did what they told them, that those people are going to paradise.", "Where is the evidence for these understandings? It's the verse itself, because the verse says conditions (alBaqarah2:62). It says: believed in Allah. Who is Allah? Allah, His description is given in the Qur'an. You go to the Qur'an, chapter:112 of the Qur'an. It says: say, Allah is uniquely one. Allah is independent. Allah does not give birth, nor is born. So He doesn't have children. He's not a father of anyone, and there is nothing like unto Him. So whoever believes in Allah: Christians of today don't believe in Allah. Christians of today believe in the Trinity, and they believe that God, the Father, God, the Son, God, the Holy Spirit; they don't believe in Allah, in that definition of Allah that is given in the Qur'an. They don't believe in that.", "And they also don't do righteous deeds, because the righteous deeds are that which is prescribed in the Qur'an. They can do some righteous deeds, yes, like giving charity or this or that, but the main righteous deeds, which are like the five pillars of Islam, for example, these are the main righteous deeds. They don't do them. They don't give the زكاه (zakat), obligation of the zakat (charity). They don't fast Ramadan. They don't pray five times a day. So that what qualifies the verse and makes you understand that this is referring to Muslims, or the people who followed in the past the religion of their prophets, is that when the verse says: they believe in Allah and they do righteous deeds. So that's how we understand this verse.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Scandinavian Man Surprises Muslim With A Difficult Question! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mf3NbKmeOt8", ((155, "2:35–3:10"), (191, "3:11–3:32"), (213, "3:33–4:12"), (253, "4:13–4:53"), (295, "4:55–5:26"),), id: 772)
