#passage(("The question is this: if someone ends up in hellfire, is that a choice, or was that something that was forced on, meaning the parents or uncle or son or daughter, whatever that person is? Did that person choose this belief on their own? Or did anyone force them, or the Creator forced them to be disbelievers? Nobody did! So when someone comes and says, for example, it's gruesome or evil, I think that person made a choice to end up there. It's not like anyone has forced them to end up into that outcome. Everyone had a choice to accept Islam. Everyone had a choice to take that Message of the Truth and to take the mercy of God; not only to accept Islam, but to have eternal paradise, to have whatever you wish, whatever your heart desires in the afterlife. But that person chooses to disbelieve. So it's not that this is something that is not merciful of God. What is not correct is that individual dying upon that disbelief.", "And this is the thing: the Prophet Muhammadﷺ, who we believe was the best of human beings in his character and his beliefs and all of that. His uncle, by the way, he was protecting him all his life, because when Prophet Muhammad came with the Message, all his people were pagan Arabs. So those pagan Arabs were trying to kill him, actually, many of them. So his uncle was protecting him when his uncle was alive. Prophet Muhammadﷺ, on the deathbed of his uncle, was doing the same thing we mentioned. He was trying to get the uncle to accept Islam, because he wanted good for his uncle. He wanted his uncle to accept the Message of Islam. And then what ended up happening in the end? He died as a non-Muslim.", "And then Allah revealed the verse of the Quran to the Prophet (Qasas28:56) – and the Prophet loved his uncle, by the way – then Allah revealed this verse in Quran saying: إِنَّكَ لَا تَهْدِى مَنْ أَحْبَبْتَ (you do not guide the ones you love). So guidance is not based on who you love. I can love someone, but they can still choose to not be open to the Truth. They can still choose arrogance. They can still choose not to be open-minded to the Truth. Then I cannot change him.", "Let me give you a basic example in this worldly life. You can have a son. Your son can become a drug addict, and you can hate that to your guts, and then he can die of overdosing. Can that happen or not? It can happen. You can love them, and you can say that's terrible. But who in the end made that decision and choice to live that life and to die in that way? It was the son. So even though we might love goodness for our family members, we might love goodness for the people around us, the people that we like, sometimes those people don't want goodness for themselves. Sometimes you want goodness for someone, but they don't want goodness for their own.", "So what matters the most is this: where are you going to end up? Because when it comes to the afterlife, nobody's going to be thinking about their family members.", "When the day of resurrection happens and people are resurrected, and they see hellfire, and they see the afterlife, and they see angels, and they see these things that are veiled from us now, which is the world of the unseen, Allah says in the Quran (Abas80:34-37), يَوْمَ يَفِرُّ ٱلْمَرْءُ مِنْ أَخِيهِ (when the person runs away from his own brother) وَأُمِّهِۦ وَأَبِيهِ (his mother and his father) وَصَـٰحِبَتِهِۦ وَبَنِيهِ (his wife and his children) لِكُلِّ ٱمْرِئٍۢ مِّنْهُمْ يَوْمَئِذٍۢ شَأْنٌۭ يُغْنِيهِ (everyone will have his own business to think about himself). Am I going to end up in this fire that I can see, that this hellfire is eternal place? Or where am I going?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Scandinavian Man Surprises Muslim With A Difficult Question! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mf3NbKmeOt8", ((370, "6:10–7:05"), (430, "7:10–7:51"), (472, "7:52–8:18"), (499, "8:19–8:52"), (536, "8:56–9:04"), (547, "9:07–9:39"),), id: 782)
