#passage(("Now the word عبد ('Abd: Slave). A very beautiful book by Dr. Bassam Saeh, it's called: المعجزة: إعادة قراءة الإعجاز اللغوي في القرآن الكريم (The Miracle: Rereading the Linguistic Miracle in the Holy Quran). He says something very beautiful there. He says that the word 'Abd can be taken from two different roots. One is عبودية, which is slavery, and one is عابد, which is a worshipper, from عبادة (worshipping). You can take the meaning that suits you. Now you're saying 'Abd: of course we're all slaves and servants of God, but we're also worshippers of God. So he said in that book: why are we taking one meaning and not looking at the other meaning as well, which is also a root meaning of the word? We worship God with our own will and choice. We are worshippers of God. 'Abdullah عبدالله can also mean the worshipper of Allah, not necessarily just the slave of Allah, or the servant of Allah.", "But you are by definition a slave and a servant, because you have been brought in this life and created and been provided for and being looked after, and your life is being given to you by the Creator. Your body, you don't own it. It's been given to you. The life you have has been given to you. That's the definition of being a slave or a servant: that the things that you have is not really yours. It's been given to you by God, all of it. So, yes, you are a servant and you are a slave. And through that slavery and through that worship, you attain true freedom. You attain true happiness, because The One who made you, The One who created you, He knows that this will work for you.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Questions The Rationality Behind Islamic Laws! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l86scGUnVXI", ((684, "11:24–12:12"), (735, "12:15–12:48"),), id: 788)
// U ML245:2-34 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("There's no 99 ways in Islam. There's 99 attributes of Allah, names and attributes, which is just the description of God. 99 attributes, and they're not limited to that, but these are the ones we're told by the prophet. So the prophet said Allah has many names and attributes; we are told 99 of them. And then he said: who understands them and lives by them, then he will go to paradise. So this is a good thing for people to know. So Allah has names, for example: forgiving, the merciful, Al-Aziz, Al-Jabar, Al-Malik, the King. All of these are names. Al-Wadud, excessively loving. These are names of God that they have meanings and attributes behind them as well.", "But when it comes to the 10 commandments, then Islam, there's no such thing equivalent to that. We have general commands that are in the Quran, like for example this idea of don't kill, don't rape, don't steal. All of it is in the Quran, but it's just some verses in the Quran that specifically mentions these things, specifically about them. But even in Judaism, it's not 10 commands only; there 613 laws anyways. So there is the [ten] commands, and you got Moses on the mountain receiving the [ten] commands and all of that, but you also have 613 laws in Judaism. So it's not like a Jews is only going to be okay if he follows only the [ten] Command. There are other things that you need to follow, but these are the most important, for the foundation, as you said.", "In Islam, there are pillars of Islam. There are five pillars of Islam. So if you want to talk about the equivalent, maybe would be that. So there are five pillars of Islam. First thing is the testimony of faith, of how you enter Islam. It's called the shahada: to testify there's nothing worthy of worship except Allah, and to testify that Prophet Muhammad is a messenger and servant. Then you have the prayer, which is the five daily prayers. Then you have zaka, which is a form of Charity that you give if you reach a specific amount of wealth, that you have to give 2 and a half%, yearly, from that savings. Then you have Ramadan, which is fasting. Then you have pilgrimage. These are the five pillars.", "Then we have six articles of faith. So these are the external actions; then you have internal beliefs, like we believe in Allah, his books, Revelations that he sent down. The Quran is preserved; others have been changed, but we still believe in them, like we believe Jesus received the Revelation from God, Moses received the Revelation from God.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Pantheist Challenges The Muslim View Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m4s4GlDTg28", ((6, "0:06–0:45"), (49, "0:49–1:48"), (108, "1:48–2:31"), (151, "2:31–2:46"),), id: 789)
// U ML370:52-69 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("Muslims, we believe the most important thing is to believe in Allahﷻ alone, is the unity of Allah; we call it توحيد Tawheed, which is you give all your full gratitude, all your worship to Allah ﷻ alone, and you do not associate any partners with Him. And to follow the Message of the Prophet ﷺ, which is what you did right now, which is we call the Shahada (Testimony of Faith).", "And we have six articles of Faith, six things that we believe, is that: we believe in Allahﷻ alone; we believe in His books, previous Revelations that have been changed now, except the Quran, the only book that is not changed; believe in the Messengers of God, Previous Messengers; we believe in Destiny; we believe in the Angels of God; and we believe in The Day Of Judgement. So we believe things happen for a reason; they don't just happen. So there's a reason for you to come here today, the reason for you to listen: Allahﷻ saw sincerity from you, and He saw that you want to accept Islam.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Surprise Shahada! Jack Accepts Islam! | Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xFtA1vLQqls", ((168, "2:48–3:06"), (223, "3:43–4:07"),), id: 790)
// U ML027:44-101 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("Do you believe it makes sense that we show gratitude to our creator? And we show gratitude to our creator through worshiping our creator. That is how we show gratitude. So if we show gratitude to Allah by worshiping Allah, is the worship for us or for Allah? Does he need it? Or is he worthy of it? Imagine you go to a coffee shop. Someone gives you a free coffee: here you go, there's a coffee. So you start saying thank you. Why do you say thank you? Do they need the gratitude? Is it going to really give them any? No, but they deserve the gratitude.", "So Allah has given us everything we have. Allah is deserving of our gratitude. One of the ways that we show gratitude is by worshiping Allah, by praying. And this prayer is good for us. It's not good for Allah. Allah doesn't need anything, but it helps us. So there's many things that we learn from the prayer, of course. It eradicates discrimination, racism, so many other things. It keeps consistency in your day, in the actions that you do.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “New Convert Questions Muslim On Lack Of Connection With God In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pwUuzBBM4o8", ((146, "2:26–3:00"), (180, "3:00–3:20"),), id: 791)
// U ML091:269-333 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("First, we need to understand what is worship. And that is where you fall short in your analogy: where you assume that worship is just praying five times a day. That's not worship in Islam. Worship is a lot more. Everything that you will do with the intention for the sake of God, whether it's good or avoiding evil, is an act of worship. This is my purpose in this existence (adhDhariat51:56).", "And now, for instance, speaking with God, what you call supplication, or Du'a, or prayer, whatever term you want to call it, is not the actual physical act of praying, prostrating five times a day. That's an act of worship! In fact, the Prophet said \"that is worship itself\". The Du'a, which is supplication or calling upon God or speaking with God, that is worship itself: الدُّعاءُ هوَ العبادةُ (Du'a is worship). So the acts of worship are many, many in Islam. Giving charity, is that an act of worship in Islam? Yes! Fasting and Ramadan, is that an act of worship in Islam? Yes! Going and doing pilgrimage, is that an act of worship in Islam? Yes! Smiling in someone's face, is that an act of worship in Islam? Yes! Sharing food with my neighbour, is that an act of worship in Islam? Yes!", "So what God requires of us is all of these things. Now you're highlighting just one of them, and in reality, you answered yourself. When you said we don't understand God, that was an answer to yourself, because it's all about first affirming whether that comes from God or doesn't come from God. If we affirm something comes from God, and we already know the Attributes of God, that He's the Most-Wise, He's the Most-[knowledgeable], and that Creator commands me to do a specific thing in a specific way, therefore that act by default becomes the most wise, the most knowledgeable thing to do.", "Now, do I need as a human being to understand every single commandment that God asks me to do? Why He asked me to do? The way He asked me to do it? No! Because I'm not the most wise, most knowledgeable person. Also, it is the testing of the submission that I'm going to do: submitting to the \"person\" Who's more knowledgeable than myself.", "Just a very basic example to be given (and for God is the highest example; of course there's no analogies we can give): you go to the doctor, and you understand this guy is more professional than myself, is more educated than myself. He tells me to do this. I don't necessarily understand what's this medicine, never heard it in my life. I just go to the pharmacy. I have this chronic illness that I have, and then the doctor said: you have this issue, go to the doctor and get this medication! I don't really go and do extensive research of doctor's degree and life. I know this person's got more knowledge and wisdom in this specific thing. So I go and purchase the medication and take the medication, knowing for a fact that this is good for me, because it's from someone who's more knowledgeable about me in that specific field.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Omnist Left Speechless After Hearing This Muslim's Answer! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yVHQD2W5S0c", ((1094, "18:14–19:00"), (1142, "19:02–19:41"), (1201, "20:01–20:27"), (1232, "20:32–20:47"), (1250, "20:50–21:30"),), id: 792)
