#passage(("The acts of worship, we do them as Allah commanded them, not due to the intellectual wisdom that we see behind it. There's a famous quotation of one of the Companions, 'Ali Ibn Abi Talib, who was the cousin of the Prophetﷺ. He said, if these actions were based on intellect, for example, you're not going to wipe over the socks, you'd wipe under the socks. It's something that we do in [wudu] sometimes. If you're wearing a sock, or you're wearing something which you call a خف (Khuff), which covers your leg until the ankle bone, it covers it, and if you're wearing that and you did [wudu] before wearing it, when you'll break [wudu], you can normally do [wudu] but wipe on them.", "So now 'Ali Ibn Abi Talib is saying that if it was based on the intellect, then you're going to wipe under, because under is what gets dirty, not on the top. But you wipe on the top. We do it because it's an act of worship, not because of the intellectual necessity, the wisdom that we know behind it.", "So just like someone can come and say, \"Why do you pray five times a day? Why not seven? Why not eight? Why not eleven? Why specifically pray at dawn time? Why is it at 5:30?\" These questions can be done endlessly. But when it comes to the acts of worship that we perform, we do them because Allah commanded them out of His infinite wisdom. We know these are wise actions. He's not going to tell us specifically the details behind everything that we do, why we do it that way. So when it comes to the acts of worship, most of them, we will do them based on that they are commanded. Some of them, Allah will tell us the عِلّة, the reason behind it.", "So even these places, when you break the [wudu], you still are going to purify them. But why is it washing the hand and the head, even though it has nothing to do with it? Because Allah ﷻ said, this is how you do the act of purification. This is how you do the ablution.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Compares Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Igzqbqr2Pew", ((1683, "28:03–28:41"), (1728, "28:48–28:55"), (1744, "29:04–29:36"), (1790, "29:50–30:03"),), id: 805)
