// U ML248:926-1040 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("And you have people saying there's no portion in Islam; you cannot interpret that to mean laziness or this. It's clear cut. And all of the companions: you have sayings like this from [Abdullah] Abbas, etc. Way later on, you have people bringing other views, and this and that.", "This is the thing that I always say: you want to take the views of the other imams? You don't want to take Ahmed, that that person [commits] disbelief? You want to take the opinion [of Shafi'i] and Malik, that say that he's put in prison till he prays, and if he's not prayed, he's killed? Are we applying any of this? Wherever you go, when it comes to the Salah, this is the scope; you cannot exit this scope.", "We love Malik, the great; we love Ahmed; we love all of them. You may follow one or the other, but in the end, as a Muslim, go back to [the Sahaba]. Even within the other, for example, you read: he lean toward this view as well, and other as well. Why? Because he sees the evidences for it.", "The thing is this: let's say I'm a Muslim man. Do I want to gamble with whether I'm a Believer or not a believer, in something that I know all of the companions have agreed upon, because later on whoever said whatever it is? You're not sure, because you've got all of the companions agree in one position.", "The important thing is this: you don't want to be on that balance. I can't imagine myself thinking: maybe you are a Muslim, maybe you're not a Muslim; these people view you as a Muslim, these people do not view you as a Muslim.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Amazing Collab| The Muslim Lantern & Sheikh Uthman Meet For The First Time| @OneMessageFoundation”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LVdIHlFWDgc", ((2192, "36:32–36:51"), (2212, "36:52–37:23"), (2245, "37:25–39:34"), (2376, "39:36–40:01"), (2429, "40:29–40:39"),), id: 816)
// U ML100:259-299 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("You need to understand: this religion is the truth. It's not a game. Meaning, when Allah says you'll be punished for something, you'll be punished for it. When Allah says you'll be rewarded for something, you'll be rewarded for it.", "Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon [him], said the first thing that the servant will be asked, servant of god, is the prayer. If it's good, everything else will be good, and if it's not good, nothing else will be. Now you need to understand that the prayer is the foundation of everything. I can fast, I can give charity, I can do all of this stuff. But the prophet said, if my prayer is not right, nothing else will be right on the day of judgment.", "So it's very important to understand: we need to have our prayer on time, the most important. You could have other problems; everyone's sinning. Nobody in Islam says you have to be a perfect person, have to do everything right. No. But the prayer, this is the thing that will tell you: do everything to make sure that this is the thing that is done 100% right. We need to take this seriously, and we focus on the prayer. We do it the right way, Inshallah, and come closer to Allah.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Doubtful Muslim Learns the Secret to Overcoming Doubts! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XG-z1myMY7I", ((768, "12:48–12:57"), (779, "12:59–13:51"), (831, "13:51–14:59"),), id: 817)
// U ML027:348-397 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("There's many people who are Muslim by name. The thing is, what does the word Muslim mean? It means to submit to God. If you are calling yourself a Muslim, you're drinking, you don't pray, you don't fast...", "So, one day there was a companion sitting down, and the prophet prayed. And then he finished the prayer, and there was a companion who didn't pray with him. So, prophet came to him and he said, \"Why didn't you pray with us? Are you not a Muslim?\" It's common sense to pray: you are a Muslim, why did you not pray with us? He said, \"I prayed with my family.\" He already prayed. But the prophet asked him, \"Why did you not pray? Are you not a Muslim?\" My point is there. Because it makes sense, if you're a Muslim, that you got to pray. That should be a part of your belief.", "Now, there's many Muslims who misrepresent the faith. They do things that are not in accordance to the faith. In Islam as well, we have segregation between genders. So, the idea of friendship between men and women, for example. So, there's many things that we may be doing that are not done in an Islamic way. And it could be giving the wrong perception to the person like yourself, or another person, that it's okay in Islam to do this, or that this is not a big deal. No, it is a big deal.", "Drinking alcohol is a major sin. It's a sin where you're cursed. The prophet mentioned eight types of people or more that are cursed: the person who makes the alcohol, the person who squeezes the alcohol, the person who carries the alcohol, the person who buys the alcohol, the person who sells the alcohol, the person who sits where alcohol is; everything to do with alcohol. How severe this is! And a curse is to be outside the mercy of Allah. It's not a game. It's not a joke.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “New Convert Questions Muslim On Lack Of Connection With God In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pwUuzBBM4o8", ((887, "14:47–15:10"), (938, "15:38–16:04"), (973, "16:13–16:43"), (1003, "16:43–17:09"),), id: 818)
// U ML248:861-871 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("I want to say something now which is important: what if I'm someone, I watch videos from the first video on the channel to the last video on the channel, and I share and I like and all of that, but I don't pray, I don't do my five daily salaat, I don't go to the Masjid?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Amazing Collab| The Muslim Lantern & Sheikh Uthman Meet For The First Time| @OneMessageFoundation”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LVdIHlFWDgc", ((1985, "33:05–33:28"),), id: 819)
// U ML222:202-241 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("You be the person who obeys God; ignore people. Allah says in the Quran: [O] you who believe, is upon you the responsibility of yourself. Meaning, on the day of judgment, Allah is not going to ask you, why did M not pray? He's going to ask you, why did you not pray? Now, worry about yourself before you worry about your friends praying, not praying. For me, whether they do it or they don't do it, Allah will ask them, but if I do it or not, Allah will ask me. That's the important thing: what am I doing in my life.", "You pray, but you keep yourself with good companions, good friends; not friends who pray and then they leave a prayer: friends who are already praying all the time. Muslims, they are there; many Muslims pray every day. Have good friends around you, cuz these people can remind you, and if they fall, you remind them as well. But the thing is, whether they pray or they don't pray, you are supposed to be on the truth yourself, for your own sake. We don't do it because our friends do it; we do it because it's the truth, we do it because it's a command of God. Even if I'm the only one in this whole planet that prays, I'm going to pray, because God commanded me, and when I die, he will hold me accountable whether I pray or not. So focus on that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Surprises A French Man Looking For Answers! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=piz443ivKUc", ((398, "6:38–7:15"), (435, "7:15–7:54"),), id: 820)

=== 14.5 Spiritual connection and feeling close to God
// NODE P02.C08.S05
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML027:2-42 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("For you to start developing faith in Islam, Iman, you need to first do the actions. So, faith increases and decreases in Islam. Prophet told us it increases with good deeds and it decreases with bad deeds. So, if I do not start practicing Islam into my life, then my faith cannot increase. We also don't worship Allah because of [what] you refer to as a connection, for example. Let's try to put it into actual terms. What does it mean, connection? What do you expect, for example? Do you expect, when you pray, some light comes from the ceiling, or that kind of noise with the music noise?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “New Convert Questions Muslim On Lack Of Connection With God In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pwUuzBBM4o8", ((24, "0:24–0:58"),), id: 821)
// U ML027:122-263 | The Muslim Lantern | 10
#passage(("It's also a spiritual connection. When you're praying to Allah, you're speaking with Allah. You're praising Allah. You're asking Allah for continuous guidance for yourself, in the Fatiha, in the daily prayer. If you have a need or whatever, and you're prostrating, you speak to Allah. So when you pray, what do you think about? I'll tell you what I do.", "When I'm going to pray, I realize: I'm doing something right now that there's an act of worship. I'm worshiping my creator, the creator of the heavens and the earth. But I'm showing gratitude for everything that he's given me. I am about to meet my Lord, meaning I have an appointment with my Lord. Right now, when I'm about to recite Surat al-Fatiha, I'm going to speak with Allah. Allah told us that it's half for him, half for us. He says something, we say something. You are having a conversation with Allah. You are standing before the creator of the heavens and the earth.", "So, when I say Allahu Akbar, of course I also understand some significance behind the meanings, which you need. So, why do we do this, for example? Allahu Akbar means Allah is greater than everything. So, when I'm saying Allahu Akbar, I'm internalizing the idea Allah is greater than everything. And when I do this, I'm throwing the entire worldly life behind me. So, when I'm putting my hands, I'm not thinking: I haven't cooked the chicken today; I'm going to meet Alex tomorrow. If that's what's going through your mind, of course you're not going to have any connection with Allah, because your mind is not into the action that you're doing.", "So this is where the prayer is going to feel different. It's where you actually internalize what you're about to do. You're preparing consciously. That's why we have voluntary prayers. Why are they there? It's a preparation to go into the main prayer. So, if I pray two units as a voluntary prayer, I'm preparing myself, because I could be distracted, this and that. I'm getting in focus mode. I'm locking in. Then, now it's the time of the prayer. I start to pray. Put the worldly life behind me.", "And then I will say a prayer, for example. Prayer is before reciting Surah Al-Fatiha: there is some recommendations for you to say prayer. Like: I directed myself to the one who created the heavens and the earth, without associating any partner, and I'm from the Muslims. So, first I'm saying: I directed myself; now I'm directing myself to the one who created the heavens and the earth. And then I'm lowering my eyes. Why? Out of humbleness. I'm standing before the creator who created the heavens and the earth. You're going to stand up looking up, or you're going to stand humbling yourself?", "Then you start reciting in the name of Allah. So, you're addressing Allah. You start asking Allah for something. Then you prostrate. You go into kneeling down. What am I doing? I'm visualizing. I'm right now kneeling in front of the creator. And then when I say Subhana, glory be, I'm talking to Allah that I'm prostrating to. So, if you put that kind of visualization in your mind, understand the meaning behind the words that you're saying in the prayer, then the prayer starts to become a connection between you and Allah. If Allah is not in your mind: let me pray, I'm going to start praying; I'm not even thinking about God or what I'm doing or internalizing it; it's not going to have an effect on my heart. And a lot of Muslims struggle with that, because they don't do that.", "I found, if I pray when I'm distracted versus if I'm praying when I'm internalizing, it's a world and night difference behind how you feel, what you feel. You also need to contemplate on the words you're reading, the meaning behind them: what is the meaning behind Surah Al-Fatiha and any other verse I'm going to read. If you do that, it's impossible for you not to feel something. But if we're doing it as robots (let's just do it), we're not going to have a connection with Allah.", "Also, the other things. If I'm sinning: I could be praying, but I could have other things, many, in my life; could be doing many other things in the wrong way. If this is the case, it's going to influence my connection with Allah. So, if I'm doing bad deeds, my Iman is becoming lower and lower. My connection when I'm praying is not the same.", "There's something very interesting. There's a scholar, major scholar in Islamic history. It's called Ibn al-Qayyim. And he wrote about prayer. One of the things that he says, that it could be a sin for you to be chasing the feeling that you get when you are praying. Why? Because you are praying for the feeling. You're not praying to Allah, for Allah, as gratitude. You're praying because you want to feel that prayer.", "There is also tradition from the righteous predecessors, companions or students back in the day. One of them, he said that I struggled with night prayer. Night prayer is Qiyam al-Layl, or night prayer: you wake up in the middle of the night. He said, \"I struggled with it for 20 years and I enjoyed it for 20 years.\" 20 years it was difficult to wake up at night to pray, and then 20 years he reaped the benefit. So, sometimes I'm like, \"Today I'm praying right now. Am I feeling something?\" It takes time. You got to connect with God. You got to start learning more about Allah. Have you started studying about Allah, his attributes, his names, stories of the prophets? This is how you build your connection with your creator. And then these other things come bit by bit.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “New Convert Questions Muslim On Lack Of Connection With God In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pwUuzBBM4o8", ((277, "4:37–5:54"), (359, "5:59–6:32"), (392, "6:32–7:04"), (429, "7:09–7:36"), (459, "7:39–8:12"), (492, "8:12–8:54"), (540, "9:00–9:27"), (568, "9:28–9:52"), (593, "9:53–10:20"), (621, "10:21–11:06"),), id: 822)
// U ML151:390-405 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("This is a personal belief: I believe so many feelings cannot be put into words, the feeling that you have as a Muslim. If I were to say something, you feel like your heart is flying. When you pray, when you truly connect to the Creator, you feel like your heart is not here in this world. It's as if my heart or my soul is already in paradise. If you could talk about happiness that you feel physically, and I'm talking about spiritual happiness that you feel deeply in your heart when you're truly connected with God, when you pray.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Grills Muslim With Difficult And Inquisitive Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eOPafAukvyc", ((905, "15:05–15:37"),), id: 823)
// U ML183:356-397 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("We'll have to ask again: what does someone mean when they say \"Presence of God\"? Because we don't believe that God Himself is in the creation or anything. But the presence of God that we believe in is the presence of care: that God looks after you, He sees you, He hears you, He protects you. That type of presence of God that comes from the word رب (Rabb: Lord), Rabb, which means He raises you, He looks after you, He takes care of you, He guides you, He teaches you. Now, if you want to use \"presence\" from that sense, then we wouldn't have an issue, of course, with that. That is the guidance that comes from Allah.", "And this sense of peace that you have, that Allahﷻ talks about in the Quran, comes through your connection with Allah. It comes in the remembrance of Allah. Allah says in the Quran (arRa'd13:28) أَلَا بِذِكْرِ ٱللَّهِ تَطْمَئِنُّ ٱلْقُلُوبُ (Isn't it with the remembrance of Allah do hearts find ease?) So hearts find ease and calmness and all of that through the remembrance of Allah, remembering the Creator Allahﷻ; you connect with Him when you come closer to Him. And Allah says in many places in the Quran that those who believe in Allah and do righteous deeds (alBaqara2:277) لَا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (They'll have no fear, and they will not grieve). They'll have this piece of serenity and peace, which is within them. That is a protection from Allahﷻ in this life and in the afterlife.", "And that's something you get while you're connecting, because you're fulfilling your purpose! You are finally doing what you're here to do. You finally found that thing that everyone is seeking. This empty place, that has been empty all this time, is now being fulfilled and being filled with the correct, right purpose, the right object to fill that gap and hole that is in every human being. That's why you feel that, you feel complete. You feel in the way that you are, of course, going to feel, because you are finally fulfilling the purpose of your existence as a human being.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Tyson Hockley Grills The Muslim Lantern With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XGBP8LOEhg8", ((970, "16:10–16:44"), (1007, "16:47–17:27"), (1050, "17:30–17:58"),), id: 824)
// R quran_013_028.json
#refbox("Reference text: Quran 13:28, quran.com (Saheeh International)", (("13:28", "Those who have believed and whose hearts are assured by the remembrance of Allāh. Unquestionably, by the remembrance of Allāh hearts are assured.\""),))
// R quran_002_277.json
#refbox("Reference text: Quran 2:277, quran.com (Saheeh International)", (("2:277", "Indeed, those who believe and do righteous deeds and establish prayer and give zakāh will have their reward with their Lord, and there will be no fear concerning them, nor will they grieve."),))
// U ML244:289-333 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("When it comes to this idea: we believe Islam is Islam, and there is no these branches that are made up, and these names that are used, like mysticism, whatever name you want to use, later on, which I believe is mostly derived from, for example, you look at Buddhism, you look at Zoroastrianism, and they take some of their teachings and then they introduce them within the religion of Islam. We believe THERE'S ONLY ONE ISLAM. That is the Islam in the Quran, that is Islam that is taught by the Prophet. There is nothing that we can take from any other culture, from any other religion, that can be useful, that is not already within Islam. And why do we say that? Because if it was from God, and God said it was perfect, then by definition, nothing a human being can come up with that would surpass that in Greatness.", "So you would say there's no such thing as mysticism? We would say there is a spiritual aspect of the religion of Islam that exists, of course, and that is very important, because Islam is a religion that is not just for the mind (which is very important, that many Christians don't like), but Islam is not only a religion for the MIND, but it's also for the HEART. It's also for the connection with God, because these kinds of interactions you have with these very simple people: in fact, if you were to ask them about these kind of theological issues, they probably would not give you that many answers, but they have the spiritual teachings and aspects of Islam that still there, which connect them to God.", "ISLAM IS A RELIGION THAT WORKS FOR ALL. It will work for someone who's intellectual, who's logical, who would like to engage in logical discourse. It will also work for the layman; it works for everyone. Now, unlike other religions, which will only work for one type of people, and will not have logical answers to give you for very important questions. And human beings are MINDS AND SOULS; they're not just minds, and they're not just souls. So you cannot satisfy one and then push out the other and say, \"that doesn't matter.\" It does matter! Because they're human beings, and that will always remain there.", "And I'll tell you, interestingly, most of the people who leave Christianity are always for these rational reasons. They go to the church, they go to the Pope, they go to the priest, they ask basic questions: the Bible says X, Y, and Z, how can you explain this? And then they just say, just believe! Or Jesus died for your sins, or you just have to believe, it's a mystery, all of these terms. And they don't give answers, and the people end up leaving the religion, because you're not satisfying the mind while you're trying to push the heart. The only way the heart would be satisfied is also with the mind being at peace.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Catholic Grandfather Examines The Teachings Of Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tyYeayKMNwM", ((979, "16:19–16:58"), (1023, "17:03–17:29"), (1055, "17:35–18:00"), (1083, "18:03–18:25"),), id: 825)
// U ML347:841-872 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("إيمان Iman: people are not equal in their faith. So we don't have one level from which everyone is the same level, no! The faith of the Prophets and Messengers is not equal to the lay person, the lay Muslim. No! People, they're levels in إيمان, they're levels of Faith. Your إيمان will only increase, will settle in your heart, when you start doing the righteous actions and avoiding the sins; righteous actions: you worship Allah, you pray, Etc.", "What is important is to know this: in your mind you have no doubt, it makes complete 100% sense, Allah exists and Prophet Muhammad is a Messenger of God. You don't need anything else. So doubt does not affect the certainty that you have, questions do not affect the certainty that you have. You've got the certainty that 100% Prophet Muhammad is a Messenger of God, and Allah is the Creator of the universe, and the Quran is the book I need to follow. I've got questions? Everyone has got questions! No one, he's got no questions; everyone will get questions, and, Etc. And then we research and find answers. I was researching some things yesterday myself; everyone is researching. Everyone comes across these kind of questions, these topics, and then we do some research, Etc. It happens with everyone.", "I feel like you are there; it's only Satan stopping you. When I heard your questions, I said: these are very basic questions; it's not something that will stop someone from being a Muslim. Because if someone doesn't believe in Allah, doesn't believe in the Quran, doesn't believe in Islam, doesn't think the teachings make sense, that's someone you need to talk to and then explain and give reasons. But you're already there. You believe it; you are in that environment where you have Muslims around you, you have Muslims friends. You've seen the practices of Islam, you've seen the benefits that they put in the community, you've seen how Allah's guidance influences society.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Challenges Muslim On