#passage(("The one who determines who's good and bad is Allah, not me. That's why there's something called Judgment Day in Islam; the one who judges is Allah. Someone can be in the appearance a perfect person for people, especially online and on social media, because you clip what you want, you put on what you want, you upload what you want. You have control. But in reality, deep down in their heart, they're not.", "And the first three people that would be in the hellfire: some of them would be a person who went to fight for the sake of Allah, and he would say, \"Oh, I did it for your sake.\" But he didn't do it for the sake of Allah; he did it so people could say he was brave. One guy who was seeking knowledge and teaching did it so people could praise him. A person reciting the Quran. These people are not necessarily doing it for God. Even though people will think these are the best people in existence, as long as the intention is not there, this is actually a sin rather than a reward.", "Because when you do it for the people, you associate with God. So instead of doing it for the sake of God, you're doing it for the people and God, or for the people only. So you're worshipping other than God in reality, in the action that you're doing. That's why it's called رياء (Showing off), شرك (Polytheism); it's a different form of شرك.", "So when you ask somebody, \"Are you a good person?\", that's a trick question in a way in Islam, because Allah says in the Quran (Annajm53:32) فَلَا تُزَكُّوٓا۟ أَنفُسَكُمْ (Do not praise yourselves). So a good person will never say \"I'm a good person\". And a bad person is not even going to admit it! So whichever way you respond: if you say \"I'm a bad person\", then people say it's fake humbleness; if you say \"I'm a good person\", people say you shouldn't praise yourself. So either way, it's a trap question!", "The biggest change of mind I have is more reflection when it comes to death. I've realized that when you go into social media, the praise of people and these things can influence a person a lot. The experiences that you have on a day-to-day basis, people can influence you a lot. And you can change without noticing you're changing. And if you don't revise yourself and reflect upon what you do, you can get lost very easily and think the new you was always you.", "This is the interesting thing about social media: if you watch your videos 5, 4, or 6 years ago, you would notice a completely different person. You feel like this was a different human being living on the planet; it was not you. So you change through time, and with social media, it's not necessarily a good change. So you notice that these things have a deep effect on your heart, just like Islam says, but you experience it in reality. You have to check yourself, revise yourself, and correct yourself. Try to straighten your ways.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The One Minute Podcast | Sneak∅ Interviews The Muslim Lantern | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8fgMbBAaheo", ((227, "3:47–4:05"), (248, "4:08–4:34"), (274, "4:34–4:47"), (291, "4:51–5:22"), (322, "5:22–5:48"), (353, "5:53–6:20"),), id: 842)
