#passage(("Why do we need to take the Biblical definition of sin, when we both agree you're not a Christian or not a Jew, and you believe that scriptures have been changed? So why do we need to take that definition of what a sin is? Of course, to go against the word of Allahﷻ, it's a sin; to disobey Allah, of course, absolutely. There are other types of sins. There's difference, because to say the laws is a very specific thing than to go against the commands as well. There are specific laws that are given in these books, the Jewish books, so there's also a difference between the law and the general speech or command of God in the Jewish tradition. It's not the same thing. The definition is the commands of God in general, to go against. That's the Islamic definition; it's not the Biblical definition.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Jew Claims Rational Problems With Islam?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8isq12mnPYs", ((1529, "25:29–26:41"),), id: 851)
// U ML057:553-617 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("Islam is categorized to major and minor sins, like for example killing, associating with God. If you avoid these major sins, Allah will forgive your minor sins.", "A sin is a sin? You have $100. You put them next to you in the bus. When you're going somewhere to work, you're counting, and you put $100 next to you, and you continue counting. And I'm a poor person who don't have any money, and I'm sitting in the seat behind, and I see the money, so I take $1, so I can buy some bread and eat it. Example A of a sin. Example B: there's an incident where a blonde woman was in the bus. There was a African-American guy, and he had a knife. You have two examples here. So, are these two sins equal? Is it rational in any world view for any rational human being to say \"a sin is a sin\" in these two cases? Are we wiser than God? Of course we're not wiser than God.", "This is what I find a defect in Christianity: that it doesn't differentiate between minor and major sins, because by definition sins are not equal, and situations are not equal, and it all depends. That's why Allah is a judge in Islam. He knows your intention. He knows your situation. He knows all of that. Allah is not going to treat that man like he's going to treat the person who took that dollar from you, and he was a poor person. This is not the same.", "So in Islam, not only are sins differentiated into major and minor sins, but also in Islam, sins are differentiated into sins against the people versus sins against Allah. Sins against other people are only forgiven by the people. God doesn't forgive on behalf of people. You have to forgive. And then sins on behalf of God: God can forgive you by repentance. So Islam is a system, when it comes to [justice], it cannot come from a human being. It's only from the creator. It's a perfect system in every aspect. As human beings, we have an innate intuitive nature. Nobody's going to come and tell you these two incidents are the same, and you're going to be like, \"Yeah, okay, that makes sense.\" It's not going to happen.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady Grills Muslim With Questions Then A Surprise Happens! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JOpckbnPmsM", ((1806, "30:06–30:17"), (1836, "30:36–31:48"), (1910, "31:50–32:15"), (1935, "32:15–32:54"),), id: 852)
// U ML167:71-90 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("Some people's mentality is like this: some Muslims think the never ending sin is not to eat pork, and they would do everything: they will fornicate, they will drink, but then, \"Oh, Haram food, no, I'm not going to eat.\" But that makes no sense; you yourself even see the contradiction there. [Stealing], killing, raping, murdering, disbelieving in God, all of these things, committing fornication: all of these are major sins. So you cannot come and pretend as if eating Haram food is the only sin that is out there.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “English Lads Shock Muslim With Their Knowledge Of Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=scOrjqCuSm0", ((155, "2:35–3:09"),), id: 853)
// U ML358:527-576 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("If you don't repent, you're punished. The repentance is to stop the act, and then have no intention of coming back to the act, and sincerely regret it and ask God for forgiveness. And there's a fourth condition: if you take something from someone, you have to return it to him; if you steal some money, you have to give it back. But Allah can forgive as well; it's up to his Will. But he has the right to punish you.", "There will be people who will enter hell fire and will come out of it. There's traditions in that from the Prophet, clear about that. People will be punished for a period of time for the sins that they did in this life. So, Muslim has not a \"get out of jail free card\", like Christianity: just believe that Jesus died for your sins and you go to paradise. No! You actually have to stick to the teachings of Islam. So, this is the thing about Islam: it's a Just religion.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Introduced To The Challenge Of The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n-flvFktgzU", ((1339, "22:19–22:46"), (1380, "23:00–23:24"),), id: 854)
// U ML422:299-314 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("They're sinners, that's it. But they're Muslims. It's not me who does it, but it's the religion that does. And the religion has specific things that can take you out of the fold of Islam. If you do not do them, then you're still a Muslim. If you do them, then we call that person not a Muslim. But the reason why you're doing these things? I would personally say it's out of your own ignorance. You do not know why these things are prohibited in Islam; you don't understand the wisdom behind them. That would be the only reason for you to do them. If you truly understood these things, you would not be doing these things.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslims Question Islam P1! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tjZje2tKQqQ", ((855, "14:15–14:51"),), id: 855)
// U ML369:42-152 | The Muslim Lantern | 7
#passage(("No human being is perfect. A lot of people have this false perception that someone has to be perfect in some way, shape or form in order for him to be close to God. So Christianity has this crazy idea that you have to be perfect: God is Holy, we are not holy, we need to be doing perfect things in order for us to connect with God. Islam does not have this idea. Islam says Allah Created us in a way in which we are not perfect, and that was intentional, because God is wise, and whatever he does there is a wisdom behind it.", "And there is a narration of the prophet ﷺ where he said, Allah is saying: if we didn't sin, he will replace us with another Creation, and that Creation will sin, and Allah will forgive them, because one of Allah's Attributes: that He is The Forgiver, The Merciful. So of course you are going to sin; that's why Allah is the one who forgives. If we are perfect beings, then we are the creator. So we will commit sin.", "Now the second thing I want to say on top of that is the idea that, in order for you to [stay] away from sins, you have to increase your faith, and in order for you to increase your faith, do good deeds. You will not have faith without becoming a Muslim. So entering Islam is the gate into having faith, and then increasing the faith through practices of Islam, and when that happens, this is how you will move away from sins. You are not going to become perfect, but you will do less sins: the more your faith will increase, the less sins that you will do.", "I had one friend: he became Muslim and he continued to drink for a while, for 4 or 5 month, until then he was able to actually move away from that. But you have to enter the house first to look at the furniture. First you have to enter Islam, and then understand the practices of Islam and try to do as much as you can, and after that you will see that your faith is increasing, and these sins that you're doing, you will understand: \"This is not pleasing The Creator, and I want to please Allah; I want to not be punished in the afterlife; I want to be rewarded, so I want to do good.\" And this will move you away from sins.", "If you are worried about, \"ok, I will become a Muslim and I will sin\": of course you will sin. Every Muslim sins; I am the first person to say this. No one is perfect. Everyone sins. The prophet ﷺ told us: {كلُّ بني آدم خَطَّاءٌ} Every child of Adam commits sins, and the best among those who commit sins are those who repent.", "The interesting thing about this idea is: this is the biggest doubt that Satan is using to move people away from Islam. The people that I have come across, most of them, I know they are ready to become Muslim, but they always have this same doubt, because Satan has his same ways with everyone. He is not bringing new arguments. He is always coming with this doubt to every person who wants to accept Islam: \"Don't accept Islam until you become perfect and not doing anything wrong.\" But that will never happen, and this is Satan's point: to make you think, \"ok, I am a hypocrite, because I will become Muslim and do bad. So let me stop doing bad.\" But Satan knows you will never stop doing bad, and therefore, Satan wins, because you will never become Muslim. So this is the psychology behind this idea.", "We are not saying: commit sins! We are just saying with Islam and with practicing, what will happen is that step by step you will move away from this idea of committing sins.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “If You Answer My QUESTIONS I will Accept Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C2LVHjA4HbM", ((194, "3:14–3:41"), (228, "3:48–4:10"), (251, "4:11–4:39"), (293, "4:53–5:26"), (331, "5:31–5:43"), (347, "5:47–6:28"), (392, "6:32–6:35"),), id: 856)
// U ML212:336-350 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("The Prophetﷺ told us that \"you are upon the religion of your friend, so look who you choose as a companion\". And Allahﷻ says in the Quran (azZukhruf43:67) ٱلْأَخِلَّآءُ يَوْمَئِذٍۭ بَعْضُهُمْ لِبَعْضٍ عَدُوٌّ إِلَّا ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ (The closest friends will be enemies on that Day (i.e. the Day of Judgment) except the pious ones). We choose who we spend time with. No one has put a gun to our heads and said, \"You have to be around with me.\" We choose who we'll be around. The Prophetﷺ told us to make the right choices. He said to see who you have as a friend around you.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Sincere Christian Speaks The Truth About Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JhnP-6KOjWY", ((1034, "17:14–17:52"),), id: 857)
