#passage(("This person says that the scholars of the Salaf have divided Bid'aah into good innovation and bad innovation. And does he cite any sources? Does he quote anyone from the Salaf, from the صحابة (Sahaba: Prophet's Companions), or the تابعين (Tabi'in: Successors to the Companions), or أتباع التابعين (Atba' Attabi'in: Successors to the Tabi'in), who said that they have divided Bid'aah into good innovation or bad innovation? There's no such thing as someone from the Salaf as-Salih, from the Sahaba or Tabi'in or Atba' Attabi'in, that said that Bid'aah is divided into two categories: good Bid'aah and bad Bid'aah.", "And before we speak about the Salaf as-Salih: where do we get our religion from as Muslims? We get our religion from the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللَّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ (Salla Allahu 'alayhi wa sallam: Peace and blessings of God be upon him), what the Prophet said. The Prophetﷺ, what did he say about Bid'aah? He said: \"وَكُلَّ بِدْعَةٍ ضَلاَلَةٌ وَكُلَّ ضَلاَلَةٍ فِي النَّارِ\" (That every Bid'aah is misguidance and every misguidance is in the fire.) So when the Prophetﷺ says \"وَكُلَّ بِدْعَةٍ ضَلاَلَةٌ\" (And EVERY innovation is misguidance), would someone from the Sahaba or the Salaf or otherwise come later on and tell you that, no, actually, this is a good Bid'aah and this is a bad Bid'aah? The Prophetﷺ said كُلَّ (every): every Bid'aah is misguidance. That's why you're not going to find what he's right now claiming. Honestly, it's a fabricated claim. He didn't even explain what is a Bid'ah. Let me explain what a Bid'ah is.", "A Bid'ah is a very simple thing. A Bid'ah has to be in religion; the Bid'aah has to be in the دين (Deen: Religion) of Allah عَزَّ وَجَلَّ ('azza wa jal: Almighty and Majestic is He). Because there are inventions that we're using today. I'm using this camera to talk to you. I'm using this computer, this microphone: all of these things which have been innovated, have been created, have been made, but they're all objects. They're not to do with the Deen of Allahﷻ; they're not to do with the religion. They are means that you can use to fulfil something, to do a specific task. So, Bid'aah is what? The first thing we have in the Bid'aah is that it has to be in the religion of Allahﷻ: you're introducing something into the religion of Islam.", "Now, when we see also introducing something into the religion of Islam, that thing cannot have an origin in Islam, whether it is a general origin or a specific origin, not عام (general) or خاص (specific). It does not have an أصل (origin). It has no origin in the religion, in the Deen of Allahﷻ. So, you've innovated something that has no origin in the Deen of Allahﷻ, and that you believe is a قُرْبَة (a means of closeness): you believe it's something that brings you closer to Allahﷻ, just like Ashawkani and others have defined a Bid'aah. You've got no evidences for this, no Quran, no حديث (Hadith: Prophetic saying), nothing that supports what you're saying. This is the definition of Bid'aah.", "Another thing I want to mention is that the Bid'aah can be used linguistically, in a linguistic sense, and it can be used from a شرعي (Shar'i: legitimate) sense. There are certain things that you hear the Sahaba saying: a بدعة حسنة (Bid'ah Hasana: Good innovation). You heard a Companion like Umar ibn Alkhattab or another Companion calling something \"Bid'ah Hasana\" or \"!نِعمَ البِدعة\" (Ni'ma albid'ah: what a good innovation!). What they mean by \"Bid'aah Hasana\" there is an action from a linguistic sense; they're not talking from a Sharia sense. So, when they say \"Bid'ah Hasana\" or \"Good Bid'ah\", they are referring to things which have an origin in Islam: something that the Prophetﷺ did, but the people have left it, and then someone brought it back; someone has reminded the people of it. So you can use that term from a linguistic perspective to refer to something not new in the religion of Islam, something that was already there in the religion of Islam, but people have forgotten about it.", "I'll give you an example now. Right now, I can wear my shoes and go in the مسجد (Masjid: Mosque) and try to pray in the Masjid with my shoes. You know what's going to happen: the uncle in the Masjid is going to get a stick and try to beat me up, because the uncle in the Masjid says, \"Why is this guy going with his shoes inside the Masjid?\" But if those shoes are completely new, clean (I bought it from the shop and it's completely clean, there's nothing on it; I don't use it on the floor, so there's no dirt, there's no نجاسة (Najasa: Filth) on it), I wear it and I want to pray with it, because the Prophetﷺ said: \"Go against the Jews and the Christians and pray in your shoes\", because the Jews and the Christians don't pray in their shoes. So praying in your shoes is Sunnah; you can pray in your shoes as long as it's clean. You check your shoes so that there's no Najasa on it. Najasa is known Islamically. Dust is not Najasa; that's why you do تيمُّم (Tayammum: a form of purification in absence of water): you do it with dust. Najasa is like urine or these types of things.", "So if I go get shoes, the uncle is going to go crazy. So, that is a Sunnah that has become a Bid'aah now, if I bring it. It will become a Bid'aah from what sense? From a linguistic sense: that the people think I'm innovating something, but it is just something that has an origin in the religion that people have left. For example, responding to the سلام Salam while you pray. That is a Sunnah: you can respond to the Salam, not by saying \"Wa Alaikum Al-Salam\", but by a hand movement. You can respond to the Salam of someone doing the Salam while you pray. A lot of people don't know this. If I do this today, they will think it's a Bid'aah. So, this will be a form of a Bid'aah from a linguistic sense.", "He says Uthmanؓ innovated this Good Bid'aah. Did Uthmanؓ call this action a Bid'ah? Did he say, \"I'm doing a good Bid'ah\"? Did Uthmanؓ innovate the Adhan? Or is the Adhan something that existed from the time of the Prophetﷺ? Is there also an instance of the Prophetﷺ making Adhan twice for a specific reason? For example, for Fajr, there were two Adhans, an early Adhan and then an Adhan afterwards. The Prophetﷺ used to say to Abdullah ibn Maktum to do an Adhan, and then Bilal did the Adhan for Fajr. So there is an early Adhan there, and still there: if you go to Mecca, there's the first Adhan and the second Adhan. The Prophetﷺ has commanded two Adhans: the one early for Fajr to wake the people up, and the second is for the prayer.", "So the جمعة (Jumaa: Friday prayer) here: Uthmanؓ, when he said to do the Adhan twice, what was the reason? It is explained in the same narration that talks about Uthmanؓ introducing the other Adhan. It says that the reason was that Islam has spread, and religion has spread everywhere, and then there were many, many people. So if you do one Adhan, people do not have enough time to reach the Masjid; they're far away. So he used to send someone to the market, and he does the Adhan there, or he does the إِقَامَة (Iqamah: Second call to prayer) there, so that the people can be alerted that this is the time of the prayer, and they can have enough time to come. So, you cannot come and say, \"This is a Bid'ah Hasana\", as if Uthmanؓ was saying, \"This is a Bid'ah\".", "And the thing is this: anything that Abu Bakrؓ did, or Umar ibn al-Khattab does, or Ali, or Uthman, anything that these four people do is by default sanctioned and given legitimacy by the Prophetﷺ. Why? Because the Prophetﷺ said in the Hadith: فعليكم بسنتي وسنة الخلفاء الراشدين المهديين من بعدي (Upon you is my Sunnah and the Sunnah of the rightly guided Khulafa arrashideen who will come after me). \"The guided Khulafa\" (plural of Khalifa: Caliph, a Successor Ruler to the Prophet): they're guided. The Prophetﷺ said to follow their Sunnah. So, if I follow the Sunnah of Uthman, I'm following the Prophetﷺ, and I've got the origin there, because the Prophetﷺ said these people are special people. They're not like everyone else. Did the Prophetﷺ tell you that you are from the Khulafa arrashideen and we should follow your Sunnah today? No! It is very simple. Those Khulafa al-Rashideen have a specific authority given by the Prophetﷺ, that they are guided. And they are the closest Companions to the Prophetﷺ. They are the best people in this أُمَّةُ (Ummah: Muslim Nation). What they do, we can do.", "Even if you assume that this is a new action (it's not: the Adhan existed at the time of the Prophet), even if you assume that this is a new action and it was not done only for that specific reason, that the people are far away and they were not reaching, even if you want to ignore all of this, we still have evidence there that the Prophetﷺ said that we can follow Uthmanؓ in what he did, which is the second Adhan.", "Did 'Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz say, \"This is a Bid'ah\"? And did he say, \"This is a good Bid'aah, innovation, that I've innovated\"? You attribute things to the Sahaba and the Salaf Assalih that they've not said. You put it in their mouth and you say, \"This is a good Bid'ah\". They didn't say that it's a good Bid'ah.", "Now, he said \"minarets\". That's the reason I explained what is a Bid'ah: because you have people like this. They bring something like a building, a structure, and they say this is a Bid'ah. Is this a part of the religion? A وسيلة (means) for us to do a specific thing: for the Adhan, we use this as a means, like I'm using this microphone as a means and the camera as a means to reach you. We use this minaret as a means to reach more people. They were using it so the voice can reach more people. And the مُؤَذِّن (Muadhin: caller of Adhan) at the time of the Prophetﷺ used to find the highest place in the ground, and they go up and they do the Adhan. Bilal used to go on top of the Ka'aba (Cubic building, House of God and Prayer's Direction), or they go on top of a mountain, and they do the Adhan, because when you are in a high place, then that sound reaches other people. This would be similar like saying that the microphone is a Bid'ah, internet: everything is a Bid'ah. If building something is a Bid'ah, then everything is going to be a Bid'ah, based on this idea and understanding.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Quran Scientific Claim Debunked? Bida'ah Is Good? | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5z_gp4XDYzs", ((86, "1:26–1:49"), (112, "1:52–2:40"), (160, "2:40–3:14"), (201, "3:21–4:14"), (254, "4:14–4:59"), (299, "4:59–5:48"), (348, "5:48–6:26"), (415, "6:55–7:39"), (466, "7:46–8:15"), (504, "8:24–9:17"), (562, "9:22–9:47"), (614, "10:14–10:25"), (628, "10:28–11:23"),), id: 879)
// U ML285:291-323 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("Here, he said that \"the Sahabi have said it\". 'Umar ibn al-Khattabؓ, he said that it's a good Bid'aah. So, you agree that in this case, you have a statement.", "Now, praying Taraweeh in Jam'aa is a Bid'aah? There is a Hadith of the Prophetﷺ where he prayed Taraweeh, and he gathered the people and he led them in Jam'aa, and he didn't do it for one night; he did it for multiple nights. And the Hadith mentions the Prophetﷺ doing it, and the Hadith also mentions the Prophetﷺ left it. For what reason? He left it because he said: I am afraid it's going to become an obligation on you. If I continue to do it, it will become obligatory, and everyone will have to pray Taraweeh. It's not going to be a Sunnah; it's going to be an obligatory action. So the Prophet left it for that reason. When the Prophetﷺ died and the time of 'Umar ibn alkhattab came, he said, \"OK, I'm going to gather the people again now.\"", "So, this is what 'Umar ibn Alkhattab did: he brought an action that the Prophetﷺ already did, and he led the people in Jam'aa, because now it cannot become an obligation, because the Prophetﷺ died. No Shar' (Law), no revelation is going to come and say now it's an obligation.", "I think these people know these things. I don't know this person, but generally, most of them, they do this with the intention to deceive you. They already know the answers; they already know the Prophetﷺ did this. But they just say it for the sake of misusing it, telling the lay people that they do not know the Prophetﷺ did it.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Quran Scientific Claim Debunked? Bida'ah Is Good? | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5z_gp4XDYzs", ((888, "14:48–15:01"), (910, "15:10–15:38"), (954, "15:54–16:07"), (979, "16:19–16:33"),), id: 880)
// U ML289:591-599 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("You'll find certain people doing certain things due to cultural reasons, mixing with different cultures and introducing things which are wrong into Islam, and that's completely rejected. Prophet said: مَن أَحْدَثَ في أَمْرِنا هذا ما ليسَ فِيهِ، فَهو رَدٌّ (whoever innovates in our affairs, in our religion, anything that is not from it, it is rejected completely).",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Popular Youtuber Asks Muslim Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S7bA8bsNs20", ((2156, "35:56–36:07"),), id: 881)

